window.ASKV={"sezonlar":[{"no":1,"ad":"Entering the Market","alt":"Who chooses whom, and why?"},{"no":2,"ad":"The Contract","alt":"Marriage, home and labour"},{"no":3,"ad":"Crisis","alt":"When the relationship is shaken"},{"no":4,"ad":"The Digital Age","alt":"Hearts on swipe"}],"turAd":{"profil":"Profile","senaryo":"What would you do?","mit":"Myth or fact?","cift":"Couples test","hesap":"Calculator","nesil":"Generation test","karne":"Final"},"bolum":{"01":"What Is Love?","02":"Love and Rationality","03":"Scarcity","04":"Needs, Desires and Preferences","05":"The Love Market","06":"Signals","07":"The Economics of Beauty","08":"Money and Wealth","09":"Equal Matches and Similarity","15":"The Economics of Marriage","16":"Invisible Labour","17":"The Economics of Children","18":"Polygamy","19":"The Economics of Jealousy","22":"The Economics of Divorce","23":"The Economics of Cheating and Betrayal","24":"Trust Capital","25":"Investing in the Relationship","26":"Opportunity Cost","27":"Too Many Options","28":"Expectation Inflation","29":"Modern Marriage","30":"Before and After Tinder","31":"The Love Industry","32":"Globalised Intimacy","33":"The Economics of Loneliness","34":"The Future Economics of Love"},"testler":[{"no":1,"slug":"fiyatin-ne","ad":"What’s your price on the love market?","tur":"profil","bolum":"05","sezon":1,"acilis":"2026-10-06","kanca":"Your partner has no price tag, but in your eyes love still has a price.","sorular":[["What puts you off most on a first date?",[["Z","Cancelling at the last minute"],["D","Checking their phone all the time"],["G","Stories that don’t add up"],["O","Rushing to plan the second date right away"],["H","Everything feeling too ordinary"]]],["Which gesture wins you over?",[["Z","Making time for you in their busiest week"],["D","Remembering a small detail you mentioned a month ago"],["G","Being exactly where they said, exactly when they said"],["O","Saying “Take some time for yourself this weekend”"],["H","Suggesting something neither of you has ever tried"]]],["What goes in the “loss” column of a relationship for you?",[["Z","Plans that always get pushed to next week"],["D","Being listened to with half an ear"],["G","Lies, even small ones"],["O","Having every move questioned"],["H","Every day being a copy of the one before"]]],["A friend is telling you about their new partner. Which line convinces you?",[["Z","“However busy they are, they make time for me.”"],["D","“They really listen to me.”"],["G","“They did everything they said they would.”"],["O","“They’re not jealous, they give me space.”"],["H","“I’m never bored with them.”"]]],["Which line feels closer to you?",[["Z","Love is measured in the hours you give someone."],["D","Being seen comes before being loved."],["G","A promise is stronger than a contract."],["O","To love is not to hold on, but to be able to let go."],["H","Routine is the silent killer of love."]]],["The fastest way to make up after a fight?",[["Z","Coming over right away and talking face to face"],["D","Really trying to understand how I feel"],["G","Making a promise that can actually be kept"],["O","Giving it a little time, then talking"],["H","A crazy idea that makes me laugh out of nowhere"]]]],"sonuclar":{"Z":["Your price: Time","For you, the most convincing proof of love is a free slot in someone’s calendar. The book treats time as one of love’s most basic scarce resources: nobody can add a twenty-fifth hour to the day. And what an hour means depends on the circumstances and on what was given up for it. The cost: it becomes easy to accuse a partner going through a busy period of not loving you. The book warns that the conclusion “They don’t make time for me, so they don’t love me” is not always true.","03"],"D":["Your price: Attention","You want to be seen before you are loved. Sitting at the same table is not enough; their mind has to be there too. According to the book, scarce resources other than money also send signals in a romantic relationship, and attention is at the top of the list. An expensive but impersonal gift won’t impress you. A cheap gift that remembers a tiny detail you mentioned months ago, though, says a lot about attention. The cost: because attention can be measured at any moment, disappointment can be produced at any moment too.","06"],"G":["Your price: Trust","Your currency is consistency: being there at the time they said, keeping the small promise. The book compares trust to capital. It builds up from past behaviour and lowers the everyday transaction costs of a relationship. But it doesn’t work like a bank account: a small delay may not wipe out years of trust, while a single big betrayal can change how the whole past is read. The cost: according to the book, a relationship that keeps asking for new proof can turn into one where no signal ever counts as reliable enough.","24"],"O":["Your price: Space","The way to win you is to not try to hold on to you. The book says people want different things from a relationship: for one person, seeing each other often is the main sign of a good relationship; for another, protecting personal space is just as valuable. In you, the second one wins. According to the book, privacy is not the same as living a secret second life behind your partner’s back. The cost: someone who wants frequent contact may read your need for space as a lack of interest.","24"],"H":["Your price: Novelty","For you, the biggest cost is boredom. The same weekend routine feels like stagnation to you, not security. The book cites research on this: sharing new and exciting experiences can support relationship quality for some couples. But according to the book, novelty is not about how much you spend; it is about the quality of shared experiences outside the routine. The cost: people get used to new experiences too, and turning life into a constant hunt for excitement is not sustainable.","25"]}},{"no":2,"slug":"kacan-mi-kovalanan-mi","ad":"Do you run, or do you get chased?","tur":"profil","bolum":"03","sezon":1,"acilis":"2026-10-06","kanca":"They say “play hard to get and you’ll be chased.” So which one are you?","sorular":[["Someone you like replied to your message 6 hours later. You?",[["K","I wait at least 7 hours too"],["V","I reply right away, maybe something happened"],["D","I reply whenever I’m free"],["S","I don’t reply at all, it wasn’t going anywhere anyway"]]],["At the end of a first date…",[["K","I say “I’ll call you” and don’t call for a few days"],["V","I text “that was lovely” before I even get home"],["D","If I liked them, I say so clearly"],["S","I leave the next step to them"]]],["Who attracts you most?",[["V","Someone mysterious and hard to reach"],["K","Someone who makes an effort and works for me"],["D","Someone who says clearly what they want"],["S","Someone who never pushes me and takes it slow"]]],["How does always being available in a relationship make you feel?",[["K","Like my value is dropping"],["V","Peaceful, I’m always there anyway"],["D","Nice, if it goes both ways"],["S","A bit suffocating"]]],["How would your friends describe you?",[["K","“Hard to catch.”"],["V","“Gives everything when in love.”"],["D","“Knows what they want.”"],["S","“Always watching, rarely makes a move.”"]]],["Which statement do you agree with?",[["K","Value comes from being out of reach."],["V","Love shouldn’t be hidden, it should be shown."],["D","Whoever plays games loses."],["S","A relationship that never starts can never end."]]]],"sonuclar":{"K":["The Runner: Scarcity strategy","You cut your supply on purpose: late replies, vague plans, a little distance. You believe staying out of reach will raise your value. The book doesn’t reject this instinct completely, but it draws a line: scarcity alone does not create value; the scarce thing also has to be wanted. The cost: according to the book, not replying, always keeping your distance or leaving the other person in uncertainty can sometimes end the relationship instead of increasing interest.","03"],"V":["The Chaser: Strong demand","When you love, you stop keeping score, and you are often the one who makes the first move. That generosity is lovely. But the book’s chapter on the love market reminds us of one rule: in a romantic relationship, two preference systems run at the same time, and the other person has to choose you too. In the book’s words: “Wanting a lot does not, on its own, produce a match.” The cost: your sacrifice is not a price that guarantees something in return, because your beloved has no price tag.","05"],"D":["In Balance: Clear signal","You don’t play games. You say what you want and expect the same in return. The book makes a distinction here: talk is cheap, because someone who isn’t sincere can easily say the same words. What makes your openness believable is that your words and actions match over time. According to the book, one of the strongest signals in a long relationship is not showing off but consistency. The cost: the same behaviour can look like strong interest to one person and like crossing a boundary to another.","06"],"S":["The Spectator: Waiting on the sidelines","You choose waiting over taking risks. The chance of rejection weighs more than the chance of winning. The book says the price of romantic decisions is not only money: the risk of rejection and emotional vulnerability are also costs. For you, that cost is high. The cost: as the book reminds us, “Not deciding is also, in practice, a decision, because time passes.” The chances you miss while waiting never show up on any bill, but that is exactly where opportunity cost sits.","26"]}},{"no":3,"slug":"kalp-mi-akil-mi","ad":"Is your heart doing the maths, or your head?","tur":"senaryo","bolum":"02","sezon":1,"acilis":"2026-10-06","kanca":"Are heart and head really enemies? Test yourself with six tough choices.","sorular":[["Two options: someone who excites you but lives a chaotic life, or someone reliable but without a “spark”.",[["R","The reliable one. The spark can come later; stability won’t."],["T","Excitement. You only live once."],["S","Whatever I feel in the first moment."],["D","I’ll meet each of them once more, then decide."]]],["Your partner got a job offer in another city.",[["R","Rent, transport, career… I make a pros and cons list."],["T","Wherever they go, I follow."],["S","I do whatever my gut tells me."],["D","First we talk about what we want, then we do the maths."]]],["You had a huge fight at 2 a.m. You’re about to say “It’s over.”",[["R","Once I calm down, I make a pros and cons list and decide."],["T","I say whatever I feel in that moment; the rest can wait."],["S","I know from past relationships where fights like this end up."],["D","I sleep on it; if it still looks the same in the morning, I take it seriously."]]],["Your friends set you up with someone. On paper they tick every box, but you felt nothing.",[["R","I give it another chance; I didn’t set my criteria for nothing."],["T","No spark, no deal. I don’t continue."],["S","My first impression is what it is, and it’s rarely wrong."],["D","I meet them a few more times. A feeling is information, but not the only information."]]],["What do you think of the saying “Love is blind”?",[["R","It isn’t blind, it just does the maths later."],["T","It is blind, and thank goodness for that."],["S","It isn’t blind, it’s just very fast."],["D","It’s blind at first, then it puts its glasses on."]]],["When should you decide to get married?",[["R","When the financial and practical conditions are ready"],["T","The moment you find the right person"],["S","The day you feel “this is it”"],["D","When your heart says yes and the circumstances allow it"]]]],"sonuclar":{"R":["Your heart is an accountant","It’s not that you don’t love; you just keep the books on love too. According to the book, that isn’t coldness: economic rationality is not about finding the option that pays the most, but about being able to choose between different goals you care about. The cost: weighing everything all the time can delay commitment. The book’s standard is clear: “Rationality is not keeping constant accounts of the relationship, but keeping the capacity to change an old judgement when important new information arrives.”","02"],"T":["Your heart is a gambler","You play big. You see the risks, but you follow your heart. The book doesn’t automatically call this irrational: if a shared life is worth more to you than what you give up, your choice makes sense within your own preferences. But it also reminds us that the feeling “I can’t live without them” in a moment of intense longing doesn’t predict the future perfectly. The cost: according to the book, rationality here is not a lack of emotion, but the ability to judge hard-to-reverse decisions in more than one emotional state.","02"],"S":["Your heart takes shortcuts","You don’t do long calculations, but you don’t decide at random either. Your intuitions are quick rules filtered from your experience. Building on the classic work of Tversky and Kahneman, the book explains that these shortcuts make decisions under uncertainty easier, but can sometimes lead to systematic errors. The cost: a recent bad break-up can inflate how risky new people seem to you. The book’s standard is noticing in which situations your intuition is a good guide, and how good.","02"],"D":["Heart and head in partnership","You don’t jump in blindly, and you don’t put everything in a spreadsheet either. You feel first, then you check. The book defends exactly this distinction: “Do I love them?” and “Can I live with them?” are different questions. Emotion tells you what you care about; reason helps you think through the consequences. The cost: this path is slow. If you drag out the decision too long, as the book says, not deciding also turns into a decision in practice, because time passes.","02"]}},{"no":4,"slug":"ilk-bulusma-sinyali","ad":"What does your first-date signal really say?","tur":"profil","bolum":"06","sezon":1,"acilis":"2026-10-06","kanca":"You say one thing, the other person hears something else.","sorular":[["How do you choose the place for a first date?",[["E","I research what they like and pick it myself"],["V","A stylish, well-known place"],["D","I ask, “I like this place, what do you think?”"],["G","I don’t say until the last minute, let it be a surprise"]]],["What do you wear?",[["E","I get ready carefully, so the effort shows"],["V","My best, most eye-catching piece"],["D","Just what I always wear"],["G","Something a bit unusual, let them try to figure me out"]]],["How much do you talk about yourself?",[["E","Not much, I mostly listen to them"],["V","My achievements, the places I’ve travelled"],["D","The good and the bad, whatever there is"],["G","Very little, let them be curious"]]],["The bill arrives.",[["E","I pay, it’s my gesture"],["V","I pull out my card straight away"],["D","I offer to split it"],["G","I smile and wait to see what they do"]]],["After the date…",[["E","A long message referring back to what we talked about"],["V","A photo or a story"],["D","“I really enjoyed it, let’s meet again.”"],["G","Nothing until the next day"]]],["Which line from them would impress you?",[["E","“I can tell you put effort into this.”"],["V","“You’re so impressive.”"],["D","“I feel comfortable with you.”"],["G","“I can’t figure you out.”"]]]],"sonuclar":{"E":["The Effort signal","Instead of saying “I love you”, you do things that cost something: time, care, research. The book says that in romance some strong signals come not from the wallet but from the calendar. Behaviour that is hard to fake can carry more information. The cost: according to the book, “Cost on its own is not a certificate of sincerity.” The other person sees your effort, but they decide what it means.","06"],"V":["The Shop-window signal","You make your power and means visible: a stylish venue, a bold outfit, success stories. The book explains this with Veblen’s idea of conspicuous consumption. A bank account is invisible, but a venue and a lifestyle can hint at economic capacity. The cost: according to the book, a wealth signal can be read in more than one way. An expensive restaurant may look like generosity to you and like showing off to them. Money being visible doesn’t mean everyone will like what they see.","08"],"D":["The Transparency signal","You play with your cards on the table. According to the book, words alone are a weak signal: a trustworthy person and an untrustworthy one can both present themselves as trustworthy. What makes your openness valuable is that you keep acting the way you talk. In the book’s example, politeness on a first date is a small piece of data; consistency observed over six months is much more. The cost: openness may not dazzle at first. Its return builds up over time.","06"],"G":["The Mystery signal","You create curiosity by holding back information. The book accepts that this can work: uncertainty, unavailability or inconsistency can sometimes increase emotional intensity. But it adds right away: “Intensity and relationship quality are not the same variable.” The cost: according to the book, people draw conclusions about hidden qualities from behaviour they can observe. Give little data, and the other person draws the conclusions. Mystery may win you the first date, but trust is what carries a relationship.","02"]}},{"no":5,"slug":"guzellik-mitleri","ad":"6 things you believe about beauty: how many are true?","tur":"mit","bolum":"07","sezon":1,"acilis":"2026-10-06","kanca":"They say “beauty is in the eye of the beholder”, but research isn’t so sure.","sorular":[["People rated as below average in looks may earn less at work than average-looking people.",true,"True. In Hamermesh and Biddle’s classic 1994 study, people rated as below average in attractiveness earned roughly 5–10 per cent less than average-looking people. Still, the book warns: this finding should not be turned into a formula like “Be beautiful and you’ll earn X per cent more.”"],["The beauty premium is the same size in every job.",false,"False. In an experiment cited in the book (Deryugina and Shurchkov, 2015), attractive workers received higher wage offers in the task where looks were expected to matter for performance. No similar premium appeared in analytical and data-entry tasks. And as real performance information came out, the effect of looks could shrink."],["People disagree a lot about who is beautiful.",false,"False, at least not as much as people think. A study by Langlois and colleagues (2000), built on eleven meta-analyses, showed that people can agree to a certain degree about who is attractive, both within the same culture and across cultures. The book adds that this does not mean there is one universal standard of beauty."],["People who are seen as attractive may also be judged more positively in other ways.",true,"True. In psychology this is called the attractiveness stereotype, or more broadly the halo effect. The meta-analytic review by Langlois and colleagues found that children and adults seen as attractive could be judged more positively. According to the book, this doesn’t mean they really are better at everything."],["Couples tend to be similar in physical attractiveness.",true,"True. In a 2024 study by Webster and colleagues that re-analysed older meta-analytic data, mixed-sex couples showed a positive correlation of about r = 0.39 between the two partners’ attractiveness as rated by outside observers. The book stresses that this is an average pattern, not an individual’s destiny."],["In real romantic evaluations, physical attractiveness matters much more for men than for women.",false,"False. In a meta-analytic review by Eastwick and colleagues (2014), answers to hypothetical partner questions could differ, but in real romantic evaluations the sex difference in the effect of attractiveness was quite small and not significant. According to the book, physical attractiveness can play a meaningful role for both sides."]],"seviyeler":[[0,"Fooled by the shop window","Most of your intuitions about beauty didn’t match the research cited in the book. In the book’s words, “Human behaviour is more complex than slogans.” The book sums it up in two sentences: Beauty matters. But beauty is not a single, objective value."],[3,"Rough estimate","You got some right and some wrong. That is exactly why the economics of beauty is interesting: looks seem to give a lot of information at first glance, but as the book puts it, “A pretty face is not a lab report.”"],[5,"Market analyst","You read well how beauty gets rewarded. Now the book’s hard question is yours: in which areas does society reward beauty, and how far is that reward actually relevant to the job?"]]},{"no":6,"slug":"para-konusulunca","ad":"What happens to your relationship when money comes up?","tur":"senaryo","bolum":"08","sezon":1,"acilis":"2026-10-06","kanca":"Money can’t buy love, but who pays the bill?","sorular":[["The bill on a first date?",[["O","Whoever invited pays, then we take turns"],["A","Everyone pays their own share"],["K","I pick a place that isn’t expensive anyway"],["C","I pay, no need to discuss it"]]],["You’ve started living together. Rent and bills?",[["O","All from one joint account"],["A","Separate accounts, expenses split"],["K","Budget spreadsheet first, then we decide"],["C","Whoever is better off pays, we don’t count"]]],["Your partner made a big purchase without asking you.",[["O","I’m upset, that’s our shared money"],["A","If it’s their own money, I have no say"],["K","I check whether it wrecked our savings plan"],["C","If it made them happy, no problem"]]],["One earns 100 units a month, the other 40. Shared expenses?",[["O","Everything earned goes into a shared pot, expenses come out of it"],["A","In proportion to income, each from their own account"],["K","First set aside savings, then share the rest"],["C","The higher earner can easily cover it, let’s not count"]]],["Which money fight sounds familiar?",[["O","“Why didn’t you ask me about this?”"],["A","“Don’t talk to me about my money.”"],["K","“You spent money again?”"],["C","“Why are you so tight with money?”"]]],["Which line describes you?",[["O","If marriage is a partnership, the money is shared too."],["A","Separate wallets, solid relationship."],["K","Today’s love grows on tomorrow’s security."],["C","Money is for spending, especially on someone you love."]]]],"sonuclar":{"O":["The Shared Pot","For you, a relationship is a partnership, and a partnership has one pot. The book cites a striking study: in Gladstone and colleagues’ work (2022), couples who fully pooled their money reported higher relationship satisfaction on average. But the book stresses that this is not a universal recipe: a joint account does not create trust by itself. The cost: with a shared pot, spending decisions have to be shared too, and that is where “Why didn’t you ask me about this?” comes from.","08"],"A":["Separate Wallets","You share love but keep your wallets separate. That isn’t selfishness; it’s a way of protecting your independence. According to the book, a couple with separate accounts is not doomed to a bad relationship. The book also stresses the link between money and freedom: someone with their own income can stay in a relationship by choice, not out of necessity. The cost: contributions other than money, like time and care, easily become invisible in this kind of bookkeeping. As the book reminds us, “Not every line in love’s budget is written in money.”","08"],"K":["The Saving Heart","For you, money is something to protect, not to spend: tomorrow’s security. This can bring stability to a relationship. But the book says money fights often come not from the numbers but from what they mean: for one partner saving means security, for the other it means putting life on hold. According to the book, a budget is made of numbers, but a money fight is often made of values. The cost: in a study cited in the book, money arguments were not the most frequent ones, but they tended to be more recurring and harder to resolve than others.","08"],"C":["The Generous Heart","You show love partly by spending: gifts, invitations, surprises. According to the book, money can be one of the ways “I love you” turns into action. But the book also writes the reverse: money can be used as a substitute for love. One partner may contribute by earning money while the other is waiting for time and emotional closeness. The cost: according to the book, money is not always a full substitute for time, attention and care.","08"]}},{"no":7,"slug":"benzer-mi-zit-mi","ad":"Do you choose someone like you, or your opposite?","tur":"profil","bolum":"09","sezon":1,"acilis":"2026-10-12","kanca":"Do opposites attract, or are your choices saying something else?","sorular":[["In what ways should your ideal partner be like you?",[["B","In almost every way"],["Z","In none, or I’ll get bored"],["Y","They should be a bit ahead of me"],["T","Same values, different skills"]]],["Level of education?",[["B","Should be close to mine"],["Z","Doesn’t matter at all"],["Y","Higher than mine is better"],["T","Doesn’t matter as long as we can talk to each other"]]],["Weekend plans…",[["B","We do the same thing we both love"],["Z","I explore their world, they explore mine"],["Y","I join their circle and meet new people"],["T","One plans, the other makes it happen"]]],["When you argue…",[["B","We rarely argue, we think alike"],["Z","Often, but it’s exciting"],["Y","I learn from their point of view"],["T","One calms things down, the other solves it"]]],["When you meet their family, what do you look at?",[["B","Whether they’re like my family"],["Z","How different they are"],["Y","What kind of background they come from"],["T","How well they get along with each other"]]],["A Turkish saying goes: “The pot rolled along and found its lid.”",[["B","Exactly like that"],["Z","I’d go looking for a different pot"],["Y","The lid should lift the pot up a little"],["T","Pot and lid aren’t the same, they just fit each other"]]]],"sonuclar":{"B":["The Mirror: Choosing someone similar","You choose people like yourself, and the picture of real couples is close to yours. According to the book, people match with others like them in education, religion, lifestyle and many other traits more often than you would expect. This is called assortative mating, or homogamy. But preference isn’t the only reason: the same schools, workplaces and neighbourhoods already bring similar people together. The cost: according to the book, matching with similar people can sometimes concentrate existing inequalities within the same household.","09"],"Z":["The Opposite Pole","You feed on difference; worlds you don’t know attract you. The book shows that the saying “opposites attract” doesn’t really match the overall picture of real couples: people match with similar others in many traits more often than expected. But according to the book, “birds of a feather flock together” isn’t a complete theory of relationships either. The cost: not all differences are equal. A different taste in music is easy to manage; opposite views on having children can create a much more fundamental conflict.","09"],"Y":["One Step Up","You look for a partner who is a little ahead: more knowledgeable, more experienced, with a wider circle. You want the relationship to help you grow. Here the book objects to a common way of talking: expressions like “marrying up” reduce many-sided human qualities to a single ranking. One partner may be more educated, the other wealthier. In such a couple, who is “up”? The cost: while looking up, it’s easy to forget that the other person has their own ranking of preferences too.","09"],"T":["The Complement","You look for fit, not sameness: shared values, different skills. The book sees similarity and complementarity not as rivals but as principles that work together on different dimensions. One partner may be strong at planning, the other at social relationships. Careers in different sectors can spread the risk. The cost: classic family economics sometimes tied complementarity to a rigid division of labour. According to the book, the long-term cost of specialising can pile up on one partner if the couple separates.","09"]}},{"no":8,"slug":"ihtiyac-arzu-tercih","ad":"Is your partner a need, a desire or a preference?","tur":"profil","bolum":"04","sezon":1,"acilis":"2026-10-15","kanca":"Do you love them, or do you need them?","sorular":[["What if you couldn’t see each other for a whole week?",[["I","I’d feel incomplete and restless"],["A","I’d burn with longing"],["T","I’d get on with my own things; when they’re back, it’s still them"],["L","Nothing much would change, just my routine would be off"]]],["Why did you choose them in the first place?",[["I","I felt safe when they were around"],["A","I couldn’t take my eyes off them"],["T","Of everyone I knew, they suited me best"],["L","They were somehow in my life, and it just continued"]]],["What if someone more “suitable” on paper came along?",[["I","I’d be scared, but I couldn’t leave them"],["A","It’s not about suitability, it’s about attraction"],["T","I’d compare, and I think I’d still choose them"],["L","Switching would feel like too much hassle"]]],["What do you love most about them?",[["I","That they’re always there"],["A","How they make me feel"],["T","Who they are"],["L","That they know everything, so I don’t have to explain"]]],["How does the thought of breaking up make you feel?",[["I","I panic; without them I’d be incomplete"],["A","Painful, but a passion worth living"],["T","Sad, but life goes on"],["L","My whole routine would change, exhausting"]]],["Which line feels closer?",[["I","“Without them, I’m incomplete.”"],["A","“I can’t stop wanting them.”"],["T","“Every morning I choose them again.”"],["L","“Everything is easy with them.”"]]]],"sonuclar":{"I":["Need: A safe harbour","At the centre of your bond are security and belonging. According to the book, a need is a general condition important for well-being: closeness, belonging, security. These don’t have to be met within a romantic relationship, but in your case they are largely gathered in this one person. The cost: the book separates two meanings of “we need each other”. In one, people enrich each other’s lives; in the other, a person has no other real option left. In the book’s words, “Commitment is not the complete absence of a way out.”","04"],"A":["Desire: The burning engine","Your bond is built on attraction: wanting, longing, not being able to look away. According to the book, desire is a general need aimed at one particular person. That is exactly where the difference between “I want closeness” and “I want to be with Ayşe” shows up. In the book’s words, “A person can need closeness, but doesn’t fall in love with just anyone.” The cost: the book also reminds us that your lifestyle may not fit with the person you feel a very strong pull towards.","04"],"T":["Preference: Again, every day","You keep them by your side because you see the alternatives and still choose them. According to the book, preference shows which wish you give more weight to when your wishes clash. Economics calls this revealed preference: the choices we make under real constraints say as much about what we value as our words do. The cost: the book is careful here. What we choose is not always what we want most; sometimes it is simply the option we can reach.","04"],"L":["Habit: A shared history","Habit plays a big part in your bond, and that is not a bad thing. According to the book, trust, care, habit and a shared identity can take up more space in a long relationship. A history built together cannot be instantly recreated with another partner. The cost: the book also draws a line: “Having stayed in a relationship for a long time is not, on its own, a good enough reason to keep staying.” The book’s question: when does staying cost more than leaving?","22"]}},{"no":9,"slug":"askin-alti-tanimi","ad":"Which of love’s 6 definitions is yours?","tur":"profil","bolum":"01","sezon":1,"acilis":"2026-10-19","kanca":"Canadian sociologist John Alan Lee sorted love, like colours, into six basic styles. Which one is closest to you?","sorular":[["What does love remind you of most?",[["E","Being struck at first sight"],["L","A fun game"],["S","A friendship that slowly deepens"],["P","A life built with the right person"],["M","Sleepless nights"],["A","Giving without expecting anything back"]]],["The ideal beginning?",[["E","A single look"],["L","Flirting, jokes, lightness"],["S","Someone you’ve known for years"],["P","Talking about shared goals"],["M","Someone you can’t get out of your head"],["A","Meeting while helping them"]]],["What can’t you stand in a relationship?",[["E","The spark fading"],["L","Too much seriousness"],["S","Trust being shaken"],["P","Lack of planning"],["M","Uncertainty, unanswered messages"],["A","The person I love being unhappy"]]],["How should your partner make you feel?",[["E","Enchanted"],["L","Free"],["S","At home"],["P","Safe and on track"],["M","Irreplaceable"],["A","Like someone who is good for them"]]],["A break-up…",[["E","If the passion is gone, it’s over"],["L","Life goes on, there are other people"],["S","The friendship stays"],["P","If it doesn’t make sense, it has to end"],["M","Very hard to accept"],["A","If they’re happy, I can accept it"]]],["Which line describes you?",[["E","Love is the heart beating faster."],["L","Love is a game too beautiful to take seriously."],["S","Love is growing old with your best friend."],["P","Love is choosing the right partner."],["M","Love is all or nothing."],["A","Love is giving."]]]],"sonuclar":{"E":["Eros: Passion","For you, love begins with strong romantic and physical attraction. Lee calls this style eros. The book explains that in early romantic love the brain’s reward and motivation systems can light up, but it adds that we shouldn’t jump too quickly to the conclusion that “love is like a drug.” The cost: according to the book, in a thirty-year marriage the ingredients of love change, and longing may give way to trust, care and a shared identity. The real question is how to carry the spark into everyday life.","01"],"S":["Storge: Friendship","Your love burns slowly but lasts long. Lee calls this style storge: friendship and closeness that grow over time. You believe more in a shared past built up over time than in flashy beginnings. The book describes trust the same way: a capital that comes not from a single gesture but from behaviour observed again and again in different situations. The cost: a slowly built bond can look like a lack of interest to someone who wants to move fast.","24"],"L":["Ludus: Play","You experience love like a light, fun, non-binding game. Lee calls this style ludus: a playful, low-commitment way of relating. You like keeping your options open. The cost: according to the book, keeping every door open has its own opportunity cost, because your partner may also think the relationship can be swapped at any moment and invest just as little. In the book’s words, “Freedom is when closing the door is our own decision.”","26"],"P":["Pragma: The smart choice","You see love as choosing the right partner: values, goals, life plans. Lee calls this style pragma: practical, compatibility-focused love. That isn’t coldness. According to the book, the real point is knowing where similarity is needed: a difference in music taste is easy to manage, while opposite views on having children can create a much more fundamental conflict. The cost: as the book warns, romantic compatibility is not a score we get by adding up similarities.","09"],"M":["Mania: Intensity","Your love is intense: all or nothing. You get restless when a message doesn’t come and struggle with uncertainty. Lee calls this style mania, and the book describes it as a form of love linked to intense dependence and jealousy. Being able to feel deeply is a gift. The cost: according to the book, jealousy can show that a relationship is valuable, but its intensity doesn’t measure the amount of love. The book’s question: what are we afraid of losing?","19"],"A":["Agape: Giving","For you, love means giving without expecting anything back. The well-being of the person you love comes before your own. Lee calls this style agape: selfless, giving love. The book also reminds us that these styles are not fixed personality types. The cost: according to the book, care done well can become invisible. The book’s standard: the fact that someone enjoys giving care doesn’t mean that responsibility should belong to them alone forever.","16"]}},{"no":10,"slug":"birbirinizi-fiyatlamak","ad":"How accurately do you “price” each other?","tur":"cift","bolum":"05","sezon":1,"acilis":"2026-10-22","kanca":"Answer separately, then compare how much you both value the same things.","sorular":[["An evening:",["Just the two of us at home","Out with friends"]],["Some unexpected money comes in:",["A holiday","Savings"]],["When we argue:",["Let’s talk right away","Let’s talk once we’ve cooled down"]],["Love is best shown:",["In words","In actions"]],["Where to live:",["A big city","A quiet small town"]],["Shared expenses:",["Half and half","According to income"]],["Phones:",["We share passwords","Everyone has their own space"]],["Bayram (religious festivals) and holidays:",["With our families","Just the two of us"]],["The future:",["Planned","Go with the flow"]],["Housework:",["With a task list","Whoever is free"]]],"seviyeler":[[0,"Different price lists","You value different things in almost every area. That isn’t a verdict. According to the book, a match in the love market is where two separate preference rankings meet, and on these ten items your overlap looks narrow. The items where you answered differently are a map you might both find worth a look."],[3,"Different currencies","On some things you value the same, on others different things. The example from the book’s chapter on money may sound familiar: for one partner saving means security, for the other it means putting life on hold. Maybe the items where you differ come less from preferences and more from giving the same thing different meanings."],[6,"Close exchange rate","You value the same things in most areas. According to the book, romantic compatibility is not a score we get by adding up similarities. The real question is which of the items you differ on are easy to manage, like music taste, and which are fundamental, like wanting children."],[9,"The same price list","You value almost everything the same way. Still, the book reminds us of one thing: there is a love market, but your beloved has no price tag. Today’s overlap doesn’t guarantee tomorrow. According to the book, people don’t have to give the same partner qualities the same weight at different ages."]]},{"no":11,"slug":"evlilik-ne","ad":"Is marriage an investment, an insurance policy or a partnership for you?","tur":"profil","bolum":"15","sezon":2,"acilis":"2026-10-26","kanca":"At the wedding table everyone signs the same paper, but not everyone signs up for the same thing.","sorular":[["Someone asks you, “Why get married?” What is your first sentence?",[["Y","To build something lasting together"],["S","So that someone is by my side on hard days"],["O","Life works better when two people are a team"],["T","Sharing life with them is more fun"],["H","To give our relationship an official guarantee"]]],["What do you think is most “shared” in a marriage?",[["Y","The home, the savings, the long-term plans"],["S","The bad days and the surprises"],["O","The daily chores and responsibilities"],["T","Holidays, hobbies, free time"],["H","Rights and obligations"]]],["Your partner has lost their job. What is your first thought?",[["Y","How do we protect our shared plans?"],["S","This is what we are here for. My income is enough for a while."],["O","We will reorganise who does what at home."],["T","We have more time together now, let’s use it well."],["H","I will check our official rights and protections."]]],["What does a legal marriage add to a relationship?",[["Y","The courage to make shared investments"],["S","A promise of support for whatever the future brings"],["O","Clear roles and duties"],["T","A celebration of the life we are building together"],["H","A legally recognised package of rights"]]],["What would wear you down most in a marriage?",[["Y","Not being able to invest in a shared future"],["S","Being left alone at a difficult moment"],["O","The work always landing on one person"],["T","Not finding anything we both enjoy"],["H","Rules and rights staying unclear"]]],["Which sentence feels closest to you?",[["Y","Marriage is an investment two people make in the future."],["S","Marriage is a safety net, for better or for worse."],["O","Marriage is a division of labour that works well."],["T","Marriage is choosing to live life together."],["H","Marriage is a written promise between two people."]]]],"sonuclar":{"Y":["Marriage for you is: An investment","For you, marriage is an investment two people make in the future: a shared home, shared plans, the long run. The book calls these relationship-specific investments, because when the relationship ends, most of them lose much of their value. A legally recognised partnership can make the message “I won’t just walk away tomorrow” more believable. There is a price too: if one partner gives up a career to specialise, that can become an investment whose cost falls on one person alone if the couple splits.","15"],"S":["Marriage for you is: Insurance","For you, a spouse is a small safety network against life’s uncertainties. If one loses a job, the other’s income continues. In illness, time and resources are shared out again. The book reads “for better, for worse” exactly like this: a mutual promise against an uncertain future. But it adds a warning: a family is not a perfect insurance company. An experiment with married couples in Kenya showed that risk-sharing between spouses can be incomplete.","15"],"O":["Marriage for you is: A partnership","You see marriage as a team that works well: each person takes on what they do best, and the household produces together. Gary Becker’s 1973 theory of marriage also studied marriage not as the opposite of romance but as a partnership that creates gains. A household does not only consume; it uses time to create value inside the home. The risky part: even if total output is high, market income may be written in only one person’s name, and the work done at home can stay invisible.","15"],"T":["Marriage for you is: Companionship","For you, marriage is not about splitting the jobs you can’t do alone. It is about sharing the things you love doing together. Economists Stevenson and Wolfers call this consumption complementarity: shared tastes, trips, projects. According to the book, in modern marriage this side may have become more important than the classic division of labour. The price: as economic need gets smaller, the answer to “Why am I staying with this person?” depends more and more on free choice.","15"],"H":["Marriage for you is: A contract","For you, a legal marriage is a written guarantee that stands next to love. The book also sees marriage, in many legal systems, as a ready-made package of rights and obligations: inheritance, shared property, social security, rules for separation. In other words, a legal tool for coordinating an uncertain future. But the book also draws a line: commitment and prison are not the same thing. A good institution makes commitment stronger while also protecting it as a free choice.","15"]}},{"no":12,"slug":"gorunmeyen-emek","ad":"Invisible Labour Calculator: whose hours at home go unrecorded?","tur":"hesap","bolum":"16","sezon":2,"acilis":"2026-10-29","kanca":"Some work never shows up on anyone’s payslip, but it still comes out of someone’s day.","sorular":[["At home, when the detergent, bread or toothpaste is about to run out, who usually notices?",[[0,"Almost never me"],[1,"Sometimes me, sometimes someone else"],[2,"Mostly me"],[3,"Always me, and I keep the list too"]]],["Who keeps track of things like doctor’s appointments, birthdays and payment dates?",[[0,"Someone else reminds me, I follow"],[1,"Everyone remembers their own"],[2,"I remember most of them"],[3,"The family calendar lives in my head"]]],["What happens when you get home from work or school?",[[0,"My day is over, I rest"],[1,"I do a few small jobs now and then"],[2,"Most evenings I work for another hour or two"],[3,"The real rush starts when I get home"]]],["When someone at home is upset, or there is tension with relatives, who calms things down?",[[0,"Usually someone else"],[1,"Whoever is free"],[2,"Mostly me"],[3,"Always me, and everyone expects it from me"]]],["A task has been given to someone. Who checks that it really gets done?",[[0,"Someone else checks"],[1,"No need to check, it gets done"],[2,"I check most of the time"],[3,"If I don’t ask, it doesn’t happen"]]],["When someone at home gets ill or needs care, who changes their work or plans?",[[0,"Usually someone else"],[1,"We take turns or do it together"],[2,"Mostly me"],[3,"Always me, and my working life has been shaped around it"]]]],"seviyeler":[[0,"Visible hours","Your answers suggest that most of the invisible work at home does not pile up on you. That may be a truly shared system, or it may mean the load sits with someone else. Think about the question in the book’s box: who noticed the detergent was running low, and who added it to the shopping list? When the mental load is done well, nothing goes wrong, so the work itself goes unnoticed. This is not a measurement; it is a tool for reflection."],[30,"Shared hours","Part of the invisible work is with you, part is with someone else. According to the book, fairness does not mean splitting everything fifty-fifty: one person can do the cooking and the other the bills, and if working hours differ, even equal tasks may not be fair. Research shows that perceived fairness is linked to relationship satisfaction as much as the actual split is. This is a tool for reflection: look at which items the load gathers on you."],[55,"Hidden manager","The jobs at home may get done, but the person who notices, remembers and follows up on what needs doing is mostly you. Allison Daminger studies this cognitive labour in four stages: anticipating a need, identifying the options, deciding, and monitoring the result. This is exactly where the sentence “I would have done it if you’d asked” comes from: the physical work may be shared while the management load is not. This is a tool for reflection, but remember: attention is as scarce a resource as time."],[80,"The second shift","Your answers suggest that coming home feels like the start of a second working day. The concept that Arlie Hochschild and Anne Machung made famous has exactly this name: the second shift. Since a day still has only twenty-four hours, every extra hour of unpaid work is taken from rest, education or paid work. In the book’s words: “Being unpaid is not the same as being free of cost.” This is not a diagnosis; it is a tool for reflection."]],"birim":"Invisible Labour Index"},{"no":13,"slug":"ev-isini-kim-yapiyor","ad":"Who thinks they do the housework, and who really does it?","tur":"cift","bolum":"16","sezon":2,"acilis":"2026-11-02","kanca":"Answer separately, then compare: you live in the same home, but do you see the same home?","sorular":[["Noticing that something is running out and adding it to the list:",["Always the same person","We take turns or do it together"]],["Keeping track of bills and payment deadlines:",["Always the same person","We take turns or do it together"]],["Deciding what to eat for dinner:",["Always the same person","We take turns or do it together"]],["Remembering birthdays and sorting out the gift:",["Always the same person","We take turns or do it together"]],["Emptying the dishwasher and putting the plates away:",["Always the same person","We take turns or do it together"]],["Booking doctor’s appointments for family members:",["Always the same person","We take turns or do it together"]],["Researching options for a holiday:",["Always the same person","We take turns or do it together"]],["Calling relatives and keeping in touch with them:",["Always the same person","We take turns or do it together"]],["Calming things down after an argument:",["Always the same person","We take turns or do it together"]],["Checking whether a job has really been done:",["Always the same person","We take turns or do it together"]]],"seviyeler":[[0,"Two different homes","You live under the same roof, but you see the division of labour very differently. As the book reminds us, “doing” a job may be just one link in a chain that runs from noticing the need to checking the result. One of you may see only the last link while the other sees the whole chain. The items where your answers differ show exactly where the invisible labour sits. The difference in perception may come from part of the work never being seen at all."],[4,"Half a map","On some jobs you see the same picture, on others you don’t. The items where you differ are probably the easy-to-miss ones: planning, remembering, following up. According to the book, when the mental load is done well, nothing goes wrong, so the work itself goes unnoticed. One question: for the jobs where you both said “always the same person”, do you also agree on who that person is?"],[7,"A shared map","You see the division of labour at home mostly the same way. That is a valuable start: when two people see who does what in the same way, talking about it gets easier. Still, the book’s warning applies: seeing it the same way does not mean the split is fair. Research shows that even an unequal split can be accepted as “normal” or “fair” by both partners. The real question is whether the picture you both see actually suits you both."],[9,"The same map","You gave the same answer on almost every job: the map of your home is the same in both your heads. Now the book’s bigger question gets interesting: does your division of labour only share out today’s comfort, or does it also change one partner’s future chances in education, income and free time? If you have many “always the same person” answers, this question matters most for those items."]]},{"no":14,"slug":"cocugun-maliyeti","ad":"The real cost of a child: 6 claims","tur":"mit","bolum":"17","sezon":2,"acilis":"2026-11-05","kanca":"Nappies show up on the receipt, but the real cost of a child hides somewhere else.","sorular":[["There is one single figure, valid everywhere, for what a child costs from birth to adulthood.",false,"False. According to the book, the cost varies hugely by country, income group, the reach of public services and the living standard a family chooses. A single figure only means something for a specific country, year and set of assumptions."],["Some of the biggest costs of a child never appear in the family budget.",true,"True. The book thinks about the cost of a child in three layers: money, time and the alternatives given up. Not being able to work for pay or study in the hours spent on a child, or accepting a lower wage for flexible work, does not appear on any bill, but it is a real cost."],["Children in large families getting less education proves that family size causes it.",false,"False. In the study by Black and colleagues (2005) using Norwegian data, children in large families seemed to get less education at first sight. But once birth order was taken into account and twin births were used, the causal effect of family size largely disappeared. Correlation is not causation."],["In high-income countries, the link “higher income, fewer children” has weakened, and in some places it has reversed.",true,"True. According to the review by Doepke and colleagues (2023), the economics of fertility has entered “a new era”. Some countries where many women are in paid work can show relatively high fertility. What matters is how possible it is to combine a career and a family."],["Cash incentives such as a baby bonus raise fertility strongly and permanently.",false,"False. According to the OECD (2024) review cited in the book, cash transfers to families mostly have no effect on fertility, or only a small or temporary one. A one-off payment covers only a small part of the cost of raising a child, which can last twenty years."],["In Denmark, after the first child, a long-term gap of about 20 per cent was found in women’s earnings compared with men’s.",true,"True. The study by Kleven and colleagues (2019) calls this gap the “child penalty”. The figure is specific to Denmark and cannot be applied directly to other countries. According to the book, it is not a biological law either: paid leave, childcare and how care is shared can change who carries the cost."]],"seviyeler":[[0,"Price tag","Most of your beliefs about the cost of a child differ from the findings in the book. That’s not surprising: most of the cost is not on the bill but hidden in time and in missed opportunities. The book’s principle is clear: the cost of a child can be calculated, but its value cannot be priced. Keeping these two kinds of accounting apart may be the most basic rule of the economics of children. A sleepless night is an opportunity cost; what that night means cannot be measured in the same units."],[3,"Budget reader","You read some claims correctly and got others wrong. That is exactly why the economics of children is hard: ideas that feel strongly true may not work the same way everywhere once they are tested with data. For example, the link between large families and education is not as simple as it first looks. The book’s question is still open: why do people, knowing the cost, make one of the biggest investments of their lives with nothing expected in return?"],[5,"Family economist","You read the economics of children very well: you know the hidden costs, the correlation trap and the limits of incentives. Now the hard question: according to the book, the benefits of raising children spread across society while the family carries a large part of the cost. How much of this burden should fall on shared shoulders? The book also draws a line: the existence of a social benefit does not change the fact that the right to have children belongs to the individual."]]},{"no":15,"slug":"kiskanclik","ad":"Is your jealousy protection or possession?","tur":"profil","bolum":"19","sezon":2,"acilis":"2026-11-09","kanca":"Everyone gets a little jealous. What matters is what you do with the feeling.","sorular":[["Your partner often texts a colleague you don’t know.",[["R","Unless they break a rule we agreed on, it’s fine with me"],["D","I don’t show it, but it stings a bit inside"],["S","A hundred scenarios start running in my head"],["K","I ask who it is and what they talk about"],["T","I mention someone too, to see how they react"],["G","If I’m curious I ask; the rest is their business"]]],["Your partner’s phone is on the table and a notification lights up the screen.",[["R","I don’t look; if I sense a problem, I talk about it openly"],["D","I don’t look, but I wonder who it’s from"],["S","I take a quick secret look, just to feel calm"],["K","I know the passcode anyway, I check now and then"],["T","I pretend to be busy with my own phone"],["G","It wouldn’t even cross my mind to look"]]],["In your view, jealousy is most often a sign of…",[["R","…a boundary being crossed"],["D","…how much I value them"],["S","…something going wrong"],["K","…me looking after what’s mine"],["T","…me still being wanted"],["G","…trust being shaken a little"]]],["Your partner has stayed friends with their ex.",[["R","No problem, as long as it’s within the limits we agreed"],["D","I accept it, but part of me is always on alert"],["S","I think there’s still something between them"],["K","I ask them to see each other less"],["T","I start mentioning my own ex too"],["G","Their past is their past; I look at today"]]],["Your partner is out with friends one evening and is later than they said.",[["R","If they’re very late without a message, I call"],["D","I miss them, and feel a bit lonely too"],["S","With every minute, other thoughts cross my mind"],["K","I ask them to share their location with me"],["T","Next time, I’ll be late too"],["G","I enjoy my evening; they’ll tell me about it later"]]],["Which sentence feels closest to you?",[["R","Rules are agreed on; break them and you answer for it."],["D","If you love someone, you fear losing them."],["S","You never know, it’s better to be careful."],["K","You have a right to know where the person you love is."],["T","A little jealousy adds spice to a relationship."],["G","Trust is a stronger bond than control."]]]],"sonuclar":{"R":["Boundary guard","You use jealousy like an alarm: it rings if a clear boundary is crossed and stays quiet if not. The book calls this reactive jealousy: a reaction to a real, clear violation is different from jealousy that keeps looking for threats without evidence. Your risk is this: what counts as a violation changes from culture to culture and from relationship to relationship. When you react to a boundary you never talked about, your partner may be learning that rule for the very first time.","19"],"D":["Fear of loss","You feel jealousy inside: a sting, a longing, a little worry. It comes from the fear of losing a relationship you value. In the study by Pfeiffer and Wong, some positive links were found between emotional jealousy and romantic love. But the book draws a line: the presence of jealousy may show that a relationship matters, but its intensity does not measure the amount of love. The book’s question fits you perfectly: what exactly are we afraid of losing?","19"],"S":["Constant alarm","Your jealousy lives mostly in your mind: scenarios, possibilities, clues. The book calls this cognitive jealousy and reports an important finding: constant suspicion and doubt do not move in the same direction as love. The digital age produces plenty of fuel for it: a heart emoji can mean friendship, or it can mean flirting. In the book’s image, jealousy is the relationship’s alarm, but an alarm going off does not prove there is a fire.","19"],"K":["Possessiveness","Your answers show the point where jealousy moves from feeling to action: asking, checking, limiting. These actions may come from a fear of loss. But according to the book, explaining where they come from does not make them right. The book says it plainly: “A feeling being understandable does not make every action taken because of it acceptable.” Control starts to shrink the other person’s space to act. One of the book’s clearest ideas is this: love can create commitment, but it does not create property rights.","19"],"T":["The provoker","To measure how much attention you get, you sometimes put a rival on the other side of the scales. The book describes this as one of the strategies people use to keep a partner in the relationship. A study in Spain found that people who reported lower commitment to their relationship used deliberately making their partner jealous more often. In the short term, attention may go up. But the book’s signalling rule applies here too: a signal carries the meaning the receiver reads into it, not the sender’s intention.","19"],"G":["Safe harbour","You rarely feel jealous, and when you do, you prefer to ask. According to the book, as trust grows, the same threat can feel smaller. Part of trust comes from information you can check, and part from respecting your partner’s boundaries despite uncertainty. There is just one risk: romantic culture treats jealousy as a sign of love, so someone who is never jealous can sometimes be seen as not caring. Being relaxed and not caring are not the same thing.","24"]}},{"no":16,"slug":"cok-eslilik-mitleri","ad":"The history of polygamy: myth or fact?","tur":"mit","bolum":"18","sezon":2,"acilis":"2026-11-12","kanca":"The history of polygamy tells a far more surprising story than most of us think.","sorular":[["Most of the human societies that have been studied at least allowed men to take more than one wife.",true,"True. According to the study by Henrich and colleagues (2012), which reviewed the ethnographic records, the share is about 85 per cent."],["So, throughout history, most men were married to more than one woman.",false,"False. A society allowing polygyny is not the same as most of its people living that way. According to the book, in most societies polygamous marriage was an arrangement open only to certain men who had land, livestock, income, status or political power."],["Today, about 2 per cent of the world’s population lives in polygamous households.",true,"True. This is the estimate from a Pew Research Center study covering 130 countries and territories. The share is much higher in some parts of West and Central Africa. Today’s world is far more monogamous than the ethnographic records suggest."],["Polyandry, where a woman is married to more than one man, has never been seen in human societies.",false,"False. Polyandry is much rarer, but it is real. Starkweather and Hames (2012) identified 53 societies that allowed polyandry and showed that this form of marriage was spread over a wider area than people thought."],["Fraternal polyandry in the Himalayas can stop scarce farmland from being divided from one generation to the next.",true,"True. According to the book, when several brothers marry the same woman, it can stop family property from being split into smaller and smaller pieces each generation in places where land is scarce. But the book also reminds us not to squeeze every case of polyandry into this one formula."],["Women in polygynous households have less control over everything than women in monogamous households.",false,"False, at least not everywhere. Studying polygynous households in Burkina Faso, Eissler and colleagues (2025) found that decision-making usually stayed hierarchical, but in some households women kept more control over their own earnings than women in monogamous households."]],"seviyeler":[[0,"Myth reader","Most of your beliefs about polygamy differ from the findings in the book. The most surprising distinction is this: a type of relationship being seen as legitimate in many societies does not mean most people live that way. Today’s world is far more monogamous than the ethnographic records suggest. According to the book, polygamy can also be studied as an institution that turns the distribution of wealth and status into a family structure."],[3,"History buff","You read some claims correctly and got others wrong. Polygamy is one of the topics surrounded by the most stereotypes. The key lesson of this chapter may help you: family forms are not independent of wealth, inheritance and the scarcity of land. Even rare institutions do not have a single economic cause. In the book’s words, one of the defining features of human nature is being flexible enough to build very different relationship institutions."],[5,"Field anthropologist","You read the history of polygamy very well. Now the harder question the book asks: to judge whether an arrangement is fair, shouldn’t we look not at how many people are in it, but at who gets to decide, how resources are shared, and whether someone who wants to leave can actually leave? According to the book, legal permission and equal bargaining power are different things. The measure does not end with the question “how many?”"]]},{"no":17,"slug":"iliski-sozlesmesi","ad":"If your relationship were a contract, which clause would be broken?","tur":"senaryo","bolum":"15","sezon":2,"acilis":"2026-11-16","kanca":"No relationship starts with a written contract, but every one has invisible clauses.","sorular":[["An argument starts one evening. What is it most likely about?",[["K","Money spent without asking"],["E","The housework always landing on the same person"],["G","What was said not matching what was done"],["B","Forgetting to spend time together"],["C","Someone saying “If this goes on, it’s over”"]]],["If your relationship had a contract, which clause would you add first?",[["K","How the money is shared"],["E","Who handles the housework and keeps track of it"],["G","That nothing important will be hidden"],["B","That one evening a week belongs to just the two of us"],["C","That either of us can leave with dignity"]]],["Your partner goes on a week-long work trip. What do you notice?",[["K","I see more clearly who actually pays for things"],["E","That I was the one keeping track of everything at home anyway"],["G","That I keep wondering where they are and who they’re with"],["B","That we don’t really talk much even when we’re together"],["C","That I can manage perfectly well on my own"]]],["A friend asks you to describe your relationship. Which sentence are you most likely to say?",[["K","“Money is always a bit tense for us.”"],["E","“If I don’t remind them, nothing gets done.”"],["G","“Sometimes I feel they’re hiding something.”"],["B","“We’re fine, but we’re like flatmates.”"],["C","“Even if I wanted to leave, it wouldn’t be easy.”"]]],["Your anniversary is coming up. What is your first thought?",[["K","What’s our budget, and who’s paying?"],["E","Am I doing the planning again?"],["G","Will they keep their promise this time?"],["B","When were we last alone together?"],["C","Are we together because we really want to be?"]]],["Which sentence does your relationship need most?",[["K","Let “our money” really be ours."],["E","Let the invisible work be seen too."],["G","Let what’s said be done, and nothing be hidden."],["B","Let us find each other inside the routine too."],["C","Let staying be a choice, not a must."]]]],"sonuclar":{"K":["Broken clause: The money box","The clause that creaks most in your relationship is money. The problem is often not how much money there is, but how it is shared. The book says it is misleading to think of a household as one single decision-maker: partners’ preferences are partly shared but not identical. Saying “our money” is easy; how it is divided is a separate question. According to the book, the gains from marriage cannot be judged only by the size of the pie; you also need to know how the pie is sliced.","15"],"E":["Broken clause: Labour","The clause under strain in your relationship is the work nobody writes down: noticing, remembering, following up. The book calls this the mental load. The person who says “I would have done it if you’d asked” may really do every job they are asked to do. But if noticing what needs doing, defining the task and following it up always fall to the other person, the physical work may be shared while the management load is not. Until this clause is written, the cost of the labour keeps piling up in the same column.","16"],"G":["Broken clause: Trust","The clause under strain in your relationship is the one no contract can fully write: trust. The book describes a relationship as an incomplete contract. We cannot write down in advance the illness, the job loss or the crisis that will come in five years. In every situation that can’t be written down, two people rely on each other’s good will and past behaviour. According to the book, trust is not an alternative to the contract; it is what makes living together possible where the contract can’t reach, and its raw material is repeated consistency.","24"],"B":["Broken clause: Maintenance","The clause under strain in your relationship is not a crisis but neglect. The bills get paid, things keep running, but the relationship itself rarely gets its turn. The book names this danger clearly: shared life can slowly turn into a purely logistical company. In Ogolsky and Bowers’ meta-analysis of 35 studies, the length of a relationship did not show a consistently positive link with maintenance behaviours. In other words, being together for a long time is not proof that the relationship is being well looked after.","25"],"C":["Broken clause: The exit","The clause under strain in your relationship is the one talked about least: the exit. Whether staying is a choice or a necessity may have become blurred. The book sees a basic difference between commitment and prison: when leaving becomes impossible, the investment is protected, but the way out of a bad relationship is blocked too. On the other hand, a constant threat to leave can also be manipulation. The book’s observation: “Having more freedom to leave is not the same as wanting more to leave.”","15"]}},{"no":18,"slug":"bosanmanin-faturasi","ad":"The real bill for divorce","tur":"mit","bolum":"22","sezon":2,"acilis":"2026-11-19","kanca":"The cost of divorce does not start at the courthouse door, and it does not end there either.","sorular":[["Even if a separating couple’s total income doesn’t change at all, the cost of living per person can go up.",true,"True. When one kitchen, one internet connection and one living room are split into two households, economies of scale are lost. As the book puts it, when you separate from your spouse, you also separate from the economies of scale of a shared household."],["Laws allowing one partner alone to start a divorce caused a large, permanent rise in divorces.",false,"False. According to Justin Wolfers’ (2006) re-examination of US states, there was a temporary rise after the reforms, but most of it reversed within about ten years."],["“Half of all marriages end in divorce” is true for every country and every generation.",false,"False. According to the book, single figures like this are not universal truths. Dividing the number of divorces in a year by the population does not tell you how many existing marriages will end in divorce. To understand divorce, the denominator matters at least as much as the numerator."],["In the US, in divorces after age 50, the wealth of both women and men fell by about half.",true,"True. In the study by Lin and Brown (2021), wealth fell by about half in both groups, while a measure of living standards fell by about 45 per cent for women and about 21 per cent for men. These are results for a specific country, period and age group, not universal rates."],["A rising divorce rate shows that the quality of marriages is falling.",false,"False, at least not always. According to the book, in times of high economic dependence some bad marriages may never have appeared in the statistics, because one partner could not afford to leave. When the cost of leaving falls, these marriages become visible (Stevenson and Wolfers, 2006)."],["A long-term study in Germany found that the income loss and poverty risk after divorce were bigger and longer-lasting for women.",true,"True. In Thomas Leopold’s (2018) study, men had sharper but shorter drops in some subjective measures, such as life satisfaction. Women’s loss of household income and higher poverty risk, however, were bigger and longer-lasting. The answer to “Who is it worse for?” depends on which outcome you are talking about."]],"seviyeler":[[0,"Quick sum","Most of your beliefs about the economics of divorce differ from the findings in the book. Perhaps the most important point is this: the first economic shock of divorce is not romantic, but it is real. Fixed costs that one household used to carry are now spread over two. Rent, heating, furniture and moving put the same income under pressure twice. Even if total income doesn’t change, the cost of living per person can go up."],[3,"Half the bill","You read some items correctly and missed others. That’s very natural, because some of the most repeated sentences about divorce do not match the data. That is exactly why divorce statistics can be misleading: a single rate does not show who pays which cost, and for how long. According to the book, the outcome of a divorce depends on which marriage you are leaving and which life you go into afterwards. The book’s question still stands: when is staying more expensive than leaving?"],[5,"Full statement","You read the bill for divorce very well: you know about the loss of scale, the limits of the law’s effect, and who loses what. Now the book’s hardest question is yours: if this marriage continues, or if it ends, who carries which costs? According to the book, a good divorce does not mean a painless one. A more meaningful test is whether the unavoidable costs are made bigger by unnecessary conflict."]]},{"no":19,"slug":"evliligin-yuzyili","ad":"Which century is your marriage from?","tur":"nesil","bolum":"29","sezon":2,"acilis":"2026-11-23","kanca":"Marrying for love is a newer idea than you think. So which era does your dream of marriage belong to?","sorular":[["When choosing a spouse, who or what should have the biggest say?",[["K","My family, because marriage joins two families"],["R","My heart: no love, no marriage"],["U","Being clear about who takes on what"],["A","Whether they can be my best friend"],["B","The moment I feel ready"]]],["Where does marriage sit in a life?",[["K","At the start of adulthood and family life"],["R","As the natural ending of a great love"],["U","When a home is set up and roles are shared"],["A","Where a friendship lasts a lifetime"],["B","At the very top, once everything else is in place"]]],["How should money and work be split at home?",[["K","As tradition and the family expect"],["R","If there’s love, the rest sorts itself out"],["U","One earns outside, the other runs the home"],["A","We decide together and do it together"],["B","We talk everything through and build our own system"]]],["What is the most important quality of a good spouse?",[["K","Being devoted to their family and responsibilities"],["R","Still giving me butterflies like on the first day"],["U","Doing their share of the work reliably"],["A","Being someone I can talk to for hours"],["B","Supporting my personal growth"]]],["What does a wedding mean to you?",[["K","The approval of two families and the community"],["R","Announcing our love to the world"],["U","The founding day of a new household"],["A","A lovely celebration with the people closest to us"],["B","The day that crowns what we have achieved together"]]],["What would be the biggest disappointment in a marriage?",[["K","Letting down the family and the community"],["R","Love fading away over time"],["U","One person not doing their share"],["A","Having nothing left to tell each other"],["B","The relationship not helping me grow"]]]],"sonuclar":{"K":["For centuries: The institutional marriage","Your idea of marriage has very old roots. According to the book, marriage in history brought together, in one institution, the passing on of property, placing children in a family line, organising care, sharing productive work and forming alliances between families. Romantic love could be there, but it did not have to be the main reason for choosing a spouse. The book’s warning: the fact that such marriages lasted a long time is not proof of deep emotional satisfaction; if there is no way out, a couple stays together anyway.","29"],"R":["18th and 19th centuries: The romantic ideal","Love is at the centre of your idea of marriage: marrying the person you love and needing no other reason. The book talks about the romantic ideals that took shape in the eighteenth and nineteenth centuries. The change Stephanie Coontz calls the “love revolution” weakened the legitimacy of marrying someone you didn’t love for the family’s sake, and made it normal to expect emotional fulfilment from marriage. The price: marriage got a new test of success, “Am I happy with this person?”","28"],"U":["Mid-20th century: The division-of-labour marriage","Your idea of marriage rests on a clear division of labour: one partner earns outside, the other runs the home and the care. According to the book, Gary Becker’s specialisation model had features that described many mid-twentieth-century households quite well. But a description is not an unchangeable law. Education, paid work and services you can buy on the market changed what this model pays off. The long-term cost of specialisation can also pile up on one person.","15"],"A":["20th century: The companionate marriage","For you, a spouse is above all a best friend. The book cites sociologist Andrew Cherlin’s scheme: during the twentieth century, marriage based on companionship came to the fore, and love and friendship between partners moved to the centre of marriage. Marriage was no longer just a union where duties were carried out. This model promises deep closeness. Its risk comes from the same place: when there is less to talk about, the main pillar holding up the marriage weakens too.","29"],"B":["Today: The capstone marriage","Your idea of marriage is completely of today: first a job, savings, a home and getting to know yourself, then the wedding. Sociology calls this the difference between a cornerstone and a capstone. In the old model, marriage was the cornerstone of a life; in the new one, it is the capstone placed on top of a life already built. Across the OECD, the average age at first marriage rose by 2021 to about 31 for women and about 33.4 for men. The price: as the bar for entry rises, people who are economically fragile may reach marriage later.","29"]}},{"no":20,"slug":"ayni-evlilik","ad":"Are you two dreaming of the same marriage?","tur":"cift","bolum":"15","sezon":2,"acilis":"2026-11-26","kanca":"Answer separately, then compare: when you say “yes”, are you saying yes to the same marriage?","sorular":[["Marriage is:",["The start of building a life together","The crown on a life already built"]],["A legal marriage is mostly:",["A legal safeguard","A public declaration of commitment"]],["The real gain from marriage:",["Sharing the jobs and the burdens","Doing the things we love together"]],["Money:",["One shared pot","A joint budget, separate accounts"]],["Careers:",["If needed, one leads and the other supports","Both carry equal weight"]],["Before getting married:",["We should be financially ready","We’ll get ready together after the wedding"]],["Living together without marrying:",["A step towards marriage","An option in its own right"]],["Free time:",["We spend most of it together","Each of us also has our own space"]],["What I expect most from my spouse:",["Sharing life’s load","Supporting me as I grow"]],["The wedding:",["Big and grand","Small and simple"]]],"seviyeler":[[0,"Two different marriages","You expect very different things from the same institution. The book does not see this as a problem in itself: one person may see marriage as a partnership of two independent people, the other as a union where almost all free time is spent together. According to the book, the problem may not be the models themselves but the partners expecting different things from the same institution. The items where your answers differ are a map of unspoken expectations."],[4,"Same building, different plans","On some basic points you imagine the same marriage; on others your paths split. According to the book, as tradition gives fewer answers in modern marriage, more negotiation is needed: the couple has to design its own institution. The items where you differ are the parts of that design not yet drawn. As the book puts it, freedom grows, and so may the need for coordination. Different answers are not proof of a mismatch; they are a list of things to talk about."],[7,"A shared plan","You expect mostly the same things from marriage. As the book observes, the state gives everyone the same marriage certificate, and couples load different stories onto it. Your stories overlap in most places. If there are a few items where you differ, they are probably the ones you will talk about again as life changes. A shared plan is not a plan that never changes. When jobs, children or cities change, the same questions come back to the table."],[9,"The same story","You imagine the same marriage on almost every item. According to the book, a defining feature of modern marriage is that couples increasingly write its meaning themselves. You are writing the same lines. One note from the book: couples may start with an equal-partners ideal, but children, pay gaps and the demands of working life can slowly pull them towards a more traditional arrangement. The dream is the same; life may change."]]},{"no":21,"slug":"beklenti-enflasyonu","ad":"What’s your Expectation Inflation Index?","tur":"hesap","bolum":"28","sezon":3,"acilis":"2026-11-30","kanca":"Lover, best friend, confidant, career coach: how many job titles can one person hold?","sorular":[["Lover, best friend, confidant, flatmate, travel buddy, crisis support, career coach, personal growth cheerleader… How many of these roles do you expect your partner to fill?",[[0,"Three or four, I have other people in my life for the rest"],[1,"About half"],[2,"Most of them"],[3,"All of them, that's what a partner is"]]],["You've been struggling with a hard problem for a long time. Who do you lean on for support?",[[0,"My partner, my friends, and a professional if needed"],[1,"My partner first, then others too"],[2,"Mostly my partner, I rarely tell anyone else"],[3,"Only my partner, nobody understands me better"]]],["Your birthday is coming up. Do you tell your partner what you want?",[[0,"I say it clearly, I don't like guessing games"],[1,"I drop hints, the rest is up to them"],[2,"No, if they know me, they'll know"],[3,"If I have to say it, the gift loses its meaning"]]],["Years from now, what do you expect from your partner?",[[0,"Security, and we'll create the excitement together now and then"],[1,"Security, plus a surprise from time to time"],[2,"Constant security and constant excitement"],[3,"The first-day spark never fading, if it fades, something's wrong"]]],["How many items on your ideal-partner list are “non-negotiable”?",[[0,"A few: respect, honesty, trust and the like"],[1,"Five or six"],[2,"Most of the list"],[3,"All of them, giving up one means I've settled"]]],["Which sentence feels closest to you?",[[0,"“Some areas of my life are richer with you.”"],[1,"“You are my closest person.”"],[2,"“You are my everything.”"],[3,"“You complete me.”"]]]],"seviyeler":[[0,"Price stability","You've put your expectations of your partner in order: there's a core, and the rest is shared with other people in your life. The book describes this not as lowering expectations but as spreading them out: a strong romantic bond and a rich social network are not rivals. But a low index does not mean “ask for less”. According to the book, expectations such as non-violence, consent, basic respect and honesty should be neither spread out nor lowered. This is not a measurement, it's a tool for reflection."],[26,"Moderate inflation","Your expectations are high, but most of them are reasonable. The book says having standards is not the problem: standards help us spot bad matches. The real line runs between having standards and demanding perfection, and a few items on your list may be getting close to it. The book's questions may help: “Does my partner know about this expectation, is it mutual, does it fit our resources, and is it truly essential for me?”"],[51,"High inflation","Your partner's job description is long: lover, confidant, crisis support, maybe a bit of career coach too. The book compares this to a job advert: we expect one person to do every task equally well. Each expectation may be reasonable on its own, but the full package gets heavy. The cost: when you expect everything from one person, every area where they fall short can look like the failure of the whole relationship. According to the book, a partner doesn't have to replace professional support either."],[76,"Hyperinflation","In your eyes, your partner is the sole supplier of every need. The book quotes an image from Finkel and colleagues: high up the mountain the view is more stunning, but there is less oxygen. Expectations this high can only be met if the relationship gets that much time and energy. The book doesn't say “lower your expectations”, it looks at the fit between expectations and resources. The book's question: “Is what I want from my partner truly essential to my relationship?”"]],"birim":"Expectation Inflation Index"},{"no":22,"slug":"iliski-getirisi","ad":"Relationship Return on Investment calculator","tur":"hesap","bolum":"25","sezon":3,"acilis":"2026-12-03","kanca":"You didn't invest money, but you invested time, effort and attention. So what's building up in return?","sorular":[["You got good news: a promotion, an acceptance, a success. How does your partner usually react?",[[3,"They're happy, ask for details and want to celebrate"],[2,"They congratulate me sincerely, then the topic changes"],[1,"They say “nice” but their mind is elsewhere"],[0,"They immediately list possible problems or what's missing"]]],["Are the small things you do (keeping them posted, remembering a detail, sharing a load) noticed?",[[3,"Usually noticed and mentioned"],[2,"Noticed, but rarely said out loud"],[1,"Only noticed when I don't do them"],[0,"They stay invisible, as if they just happen by themselves"]]],["When it comes to your own goals (education, health, work, a hobby), where is this relationship taking you?",[[3,"Closer to the person I want to be"],[2,"It supports me, but doesn't change much"],[1,"My goals are in the shadow of the relationship"],[0,"I dropped my goals “for the sake of us”"]]],["When did you last do something new together (a place, a hobby, an experience)?",[[3,"In the last few weeks"],[2,"In the last few months"],[1,"I can barely remember"],[0,"All our shared memories are in the past"]]],["Looking at the long run, who gives what in the relationship?",[[3,"It shifts over time, but it balances out"],[2,"A bit uneven, but we both see it"],[1,"Usually the same side gives"],[0,"Always the same side gives, and we can't talk about it"]]],["Where does most of the energy spent on the relationship go?",[[3,"Building new things, making plans together"],[2,"Keeping the daily routine running"],[1,"Calming down the same old arguments"],[0,"Damage repair, there's no energy left for anything else"]]]],"seviyeler":[[0,"Warning light","Your answers suggest that most of the effort spent on the relationship goes into repairs, or that what is given stays invisible. The book sets a threshold here: the balance sheet of a shared life may never be perfectly even, but if it keeps recording losses on one side, the investment metaphor starts flashing a warning. This is not a verdict, it's a tool for reflection. The question at the start of the chapter: which investments truly nourish a relationship?"],[26,"Account needs maintenance","Your relationship works, but its returns seem to be shrinking. According to the book, before a relationship ends there is sometimes no dramatic crisis, just long-term neglect. The bills get paid, things run, but shared life can slowly turn into a logistics company. The cost: while the relationship keeps doing its job, closeness can quietly fade. The book looks for the fix not in grand gestures but in small behaviours repeated hundreds of times."],[51,"Steady returns","Your relationship adds value to your life: small contributions are seen and the balance is mostly kept. The book describes caring for a relationship not as constant spending but as regular investment that protects the partnership's use value. One warning: some returns of a relationship don't renew themselves. When no new shared experiences are created, all the memories gradually belong to the past. Sometimes investing means adding new material to the relationship."],[76,"Compound returns","Your relationship works like insurance on hard days and like a partnership that multiplies returns on good days. In the book's words, a good partner is someone who can carry not only our load but also our growth. Still, a high score is no guarantee. According to the book, some of the highest returns in a romantic relationship can't be turned into numbers. This index doesn't measure them either, it only invites you to think about them."]],"birim":"Return Index"},{"no":23,"slug":"batik-maliyet","ad":"Sunk cost: are you in a relationship that should end?","tur":"senaryo","bolum":"25","sezon":3,"acilis":"2026-11-26","kanca":"How much does what you gave in the past control the decision you make today?","sorular":[["A friend is talking about their seven-year relationship: “I'm not happy, but I've given it so many years.” What do you say?",[["G","“Seven years is a lot, don't give up so fast.”"],["S","“Those years are gone, don't count them.”"],["A","“Look at which of those years still carries something today.”"],["Y","“How do you want to spend the next seven years?”"]]],["Months ago you planned an expensive holiday together and paid for it. Now things are tense between you and the date is getting close.",[["G","It's paid for, so we go, it shouldn't go to waste"],["S","The money is already gone, it shouldn't affect the decision at all"],["A","First I check whether any part of it is refundable"],["Y","I also weigh how else I could spend that week"]]],["What would scare you most about a relationship ending?",[["G","That everything I gave would be wasted"],["S","Getting stuck in the past and not turning the page"],["A","Our shared home, friends and routine falling apart"],["Y","Staying in the wrong life and missing other chances"]]],["You moved to another city for someone you've been with for a long time. Now things aren't what they used to be. What goes through your mind?",[["G","“I gave up so much for them, I can't back out now.”"],["S","“The move is in the past, it doesn't affect today's decision.”"],["A","“The job I lost is in the past, but my income and friends here today are real.”"],["Y","“I can't help wondering where I'd be today if I hadn't gone.”"]]],["What does the sentence “You've given it so many years, how can you walk away now?” mean to you?",[["G","A fair question, effort shouldn't be thrown away lightly"],["S","Meaningless, past years can't be the measure of a decision"],["A","Half true, some of those years still shape today"],["Y","The real question isn't the years given, but the years still to come"]]],["If a relationship ended, what would you think about those years?",[["G","All that effort should have led somewhere"],["S","A page has closed, I don't look back"],["A","Even if it ended, the good parts and what I learned stay with me"],["Y","I'd wonder what would have happened if I'd taken another path"]]]],"sonuclar":{"G":["Guardian of the past","For you, effort that was given can't easily be erased, and that is part of loyalty. Economics calls part of this a sunk cost: whatever you decide today, it won't come back. The cost: deciding only so that “all those years aren't wasted” can mean choosing an option that's bad for today just to rescue the past. That doesn't mean staying is wrong. The book's question: if I feel I have to continue, is it “only because I gave so much in the past”?","25"],"S":["Clean slate","You don't get stuck in the past: what's done is done, and decisions are made for today. That's strong immunity against the sunk cost fallacy. But the book also points to the other extreme: wiping out the whole history of a relationship as “sunk cost, forget it” is just as misleading as surrendering to the past. Shared children, a home, social networks were built in the past but still shape today. The risk is writing real ties off the books too.","25"],"A":["The one who tells them apart","You neither worship the past nor throw it away. You try to separate what is sunk cost that won't come back from what is relationship-specific capital that still shapes today. That is exactly the distinction the book suggests. The hard part: the book admits that separating the two perfectly in real life isn't easy. And it reminds us: an investment that has ended and a worthless past are not the same thing.","22"],"Y":["Eyes on tomorrow","You weigh decisions against the years ahead, not the years behind. In economic terms, you look at opportunity cost: which lives am I giving up with this choice? That's a good compass against the sunk cost trap. But there's a risk: the lives you didn't choose always look brighter, because you never lived their costs. The book's warning: “Real life competes with all its costs, an imagined life with only its best scenes.”","26"]}},{"no":24,"slug":"neye-mal-oldu","ad":"What has this relationship cost you?","tur":"hesap","bolum":"26","sezon":3,"acilis":"2026-12-10","kanca":"Every “yes” carries a few silent “no”s. What were yours?","sorular":[["Did you give up an opportunity at work or in your studies for this relationship?",[[0,"No, it was never needed"],[1,"A small opportunity"],[2,"An important offer"],[3,"I largely changed my career direction"]]],["How were the city and home you live in chosen?",[[0,"It's where I'd have chosen anyway"],[1,"We chose together, we both bent a little"],[2,"Mostly based on their needs"],[3,"I moved for them, I wouldn't have gone on my own"]]],["What happened to the friends and hobbies you had before the relationship?",[[0,"They're still there as they were"],[1,"They've thinned out a bit"],[2,"They've mostly turned into shared friends"],[3,"I've left most of them behind"]]],["The hours in a week that are just for yourself…",[[0,"Same as before the relationship"],[1,"A little fewer"],[2,"Clearly fewer"],[3,"Almost none left"]]],["In shared decisions (moving, holidays, big purchases), whose preference becomes the default?",[[0,"We genuinely decide together"],[1,"It changes from time to time"],[2,"Usually theirs"],[3,"Almost always theirs"]]],["Are the things you gave up talked about in the relationship?",[[0,"I haven't given up anything important"],[1,"Yes, they're seen and appreciated"],[2,"Not really talked about"],[3,"Never seen, they're taken for granted"]]]],"seviyeler":[[0,"Light luggage","So far you haven't given up anything big for this relationship, or what you gave up doesn't weigh on you much. Still, opportunity cost is never zero. In the book's words, every “yes” quietly says “no” to some other possibilities. A low price doesn't mean the relationship is worthless, it may simply mean two lives fit together well. This is not a measurement, it's a tool for reflection."],[26,"Visible price","This relationship has cost you some things: some time, some flexibility, maybe an opportunity. According to the book, a costly choice is not a wrong choice. On the contrary, what we give up for what shows what we value. The thing to watch is that the costs don't always pile up on the same side. The book's question: “Who gave up which opportunity, and how has that loss been made up for over time?”"],[51,"High price","You've given up serious things for this relationship: a city, a job, a circle of friends, or time of your own. The book reminds us: a job you turned down doesn't appear on any payslip, but it can stay in the relationship's memory. A high price doesn't mean a wrong choice. The risk is that unseen sacrifice slowly turns into resentment. The book's answer is not to put a price tag on every sacrifice, but to be able to talk about the invisible losers of big decisions."],[76,"Heavy price","This relationship has largely set the direction of your life, and what you gave up is big. The book says this is not a wrong choice in itself: a good life is not a life without opportunity costs, but one where you find the life you chose worth living despite what you gave up. But if the costs always land on the same person, that's a distribution problem. The book's standard: the missed opportunities should be as visible as the costs of shared life."]],"birim":"Opportunity Cost Index"},{"no":25,"slug":"cok-secenek","ad":"Are too many options making you unhappy?","tur":"profil","bolum":"27","sezon":3,"acilis":"2026-12-14","kanca":"As your options multiply, do you choose better, or does choosing just get harder?","sorular":[["You see fifty new profiles on a dating app. What do you do?",[["M","I look at all of them, the best might be the last one"],["Y","I start chatting with the first few that feel right"],["F","First I filter them by my must-haves"],["A","I chat with a few and don't close any doors"],["B","I keep in mind one feature I liked in each of them"]]],["At a restaurant, the menu runs for pages.",[["M","I read all of it, I won't decide until I find the best"],["Y","I stop at the first thing that looks good"],["F","I cross out what I won't eat and pick from what's left"],["A","We order a few to share and see how it goes"],["B","After ordering, I look at what the next table got"]]],["Your relationship is going well. What crosses your mind most?",[["M","“Is there someone better out there?”"],["Y","“So glad this was so easy.”"],["F","“Everything that matters to me is in place.”"],["A","“Good for now, let's see what happens.”"],["B","“If only they had this quality too.”"]]],["After you make a big decision (a home, a job, a partner)…",[["M","I keep thinking about the other options"],["Y","The matter is closed, I look ahead"],["F","If it meets my criteria, I'm at peace"],["A","Knowing I can go back makes me feel better"],["B","I compare it with other people's choices"]]],["A friend wants to introduce you to someone.",[["M","First I'd like to hear who else is out there"],["Y","Sure, if they're nice, why not"],["F","First I ask a few basics: age, city, what they want from life"],["A","I'll meet them, but I promise nothing to anyone"],["B","While listening, I compare them with my exes"]]],["Which sentence feels closest to you?",[["M","When the best exists, I won't settle for second best."],["Y","Good enough is often the best."],["F","If you know what you want, you get less tired."],["A","As long as the doors stay open, I'm free."],["B","Every person is missing something different."]]]],"sonuclar":{"M":["Chasing the best","You don't settle for good enough, you look for the best that's within reach. The book calls this the maximising tendency. In studies by Schwartz and colleagues, this tendency was linked to more regret and less satisfaction with choices, though the book notes these findings are debated. In romantic choice it's even harder, because there is no one-dimensional ranking of “the best partner”. The risk: each new option can push the end of the search a little further away.","27"],"Y":["Good enough","When you find what's “right for me”, you can stop searching. Because you don't put every new candidate back into the competition, lots of options don't wear you out much. This may be the style least affected by the decision burden the book describes. Still, the book gives a warning: an easy decision and a good life are not the same thing. According to the book, some things about a person can only be learned over time.","27"],"F":["The filterer","You know what you want and you rule out the mismatches early. According to the book, clear preferences lower search costs, so a large set of options may even work in your favour. The risk: the more filters you add, the sooner you may lose the chance of an unexpected match with people you haven't met yet. Easy-to-filter traits like height, age and job can push aside things that only show over time, like humour or sensitivity.","27"],"A":["Open door","You like keeping your options open. That flexibility has value; economists call it option value. But the book's warning is this: in romantic life, the price of keeping every option alive may be that your partner doesn't invest in you as much either, because they also feel the relationship could be swapped for another option at any moment. In the book's words: “Freedom is when closing the door is our own decision.”","26"],"B":["The imaginary rival","Even after choosing, you keep comparing: one person's humour, another's romance, someone else's success. Comparison can be useful, a good habit you see in another couple can improve your own relationship. But the book points to a trap: if you build a composite partner in your head from everyone's strongest traits, a real person can never win. Abundance creates the toughest rival of all: the perfect alternative who never existed.","27"]}},{"no":26,"slug":"guven-sermayesi","ad":"Is your Trust Capital in the black or in the red?","tur":"hesap","bolum":"24","sezon":3,"acilis":"2026-12-17","kanca":"When trust works well, it's invisible. So how much of it have you built up?","sorular":[["How often does your partner keep their small promises (“I'll call”, “I'll come”, “I'll sort it out”)?",[[3,"Almost always"],[2,"Most of the time"],[1,"About half the time"],[0,"Rarely"]]],["What happened to a secret or a vulnerable moment you shared with them?",[[3,"They protected it and never brought it up"],[2,"They protected it, but hinted at it once"],[1,"They've used it in an argument"],[0,"I found out they shared it with others"]]],["When a problem comes up, your partner usually…",[[3,"Is there and takes responsibility"],[2,"Shows up, even if a bit late"],[1,"Disappears, then comes back"],[0,"Blames someone else"]]],["When your partner makes a mistake, they…",[[3,"Tell me on their own"],[2,"Explain honestly when asked"],[1,"Play it down first, then admit it"],[0,"Deny it until there's proof"]]],["When there's a delay or an unclear message, what do you do?",[[3,"I don't think much of it, there's an explanation"],[2,"I wonder, I ask, it passes"],[1,"It sticks in my head and I feel the urge to check"],[0,"I can't relax until I've verified it"]]],["On important matters (money, health, your shared future), do you keep each other informed?",[[3,"Openly"],[2,"Mostly, some things get said in time"],[1,"I've found out some important things later"],[0,"I feel I'm making decisions with missing information"]]]],"seviyeler":[[0,"In the red","Your answers suggest your trust account is currently in the red: the need to verify is high, and promises carry little information. The book doesn't see this as a personal flaw: earned suspicion can also be healthy information processing. The cost of low trust is constant checking, verifying and defending. According to the book, if behaviour broke the trust, behaviour must also repair it. Where someone's safety is under threat, the book puts protecting that person first."],[26,"Fragile balance","Your trust account is open but fragile: some promises are kept, some doubts remain. The book sees trust not as a single score but as a set of expectations that differ by area. You can trust someone's faithfulness and still not trust their punctuality. The risk is that small repeats turn into a pattern. Then the question shifts from “What happened this time?” to “How much does this person's word tell me about the future?”"],[51,"In credit","Your trust account is in credit: promises are mostly kept, and nobody disappears when a problem comes up. The book explains its value through transaction costs: a couple who trust each other don't have to verify every piece of information or investigate every delay. Still, this capital doesn't work mechanically like money in the bank. A small delay may not wipe out years of trust, but a single big betrayal can change how the whole past is read."],[76,"Strong capital","Your trust capital is strong: actions back up words, and your vulnerability is protected. The book sees trust's biggest return not so much as peace of mind but as room to move. You can spend time on your work, your friends and yourself without staying on the defensive. One warning: the book's goal is not maximum trust but a level of trust that matches behaviour. In the book's words, good trust grows stronger not despite the evidence but because of it. This index is also a tool for reflection."]],"birim":"Trust Capital Index"},{"no":27,"slug":"aldatma-mitleri","ad":"6 common beliefs about cheating: how many are true?","tur":"mit","bolum":"23","sezon":3,"acilis":"2026-12-21","kanca":"Everyone has a firm opinion about cheating, but the data isn't nearly as sure.","sorular":[["Once a cheater, always a cheater: someone who cheated once will definitely cheat in their next relationship.",false,"False. In a study by Knopp and colleagues that followed 484 adults across two consecutive relationships, people who reported infidelity in their first relationship were about three times more likely to report it in the next one. But the book stresses that this is a difference in risk, not destiny: three times more likely is not one hundred per cent certain."],["Cheating doesn't happen in a happy relationship; if someone was cheated on, the relationship was already bad.",false,"False. Low relationship satisfaction goes together with infidelity in some studies, but the cause can run the other way too: infidelity itself can lower satisfaction. According to the book, many people who are unhappy never cheat, and some who say they're happy may still engage in behaviour outside the relationship."],["People don't always draw the line in the same place on what counts as cheating.",true,"True. On the scale by Wilson and colleagues, behaviours were grouped as “explicit”, “deceptive” and “ambiguous”, and opinions split more on the ambiguous ones. The book's summary: people largely agree on the core of what cheating means, but can differ seriously at its edges."],["A debt or spending deliberately hidden from a partner can also count as a kind of infidelity.",true,"True. Garbinsky and colleagues call this financial infidelity: doing something with money that you expect your partner wouldn't approve of, and deliberately hiding it from them. According to the book, not reporting every personal purchase doesn't count; the key element is the gap between an expected shared rule and deliberate secrecy."],["Once cheating comes out, the relationship can't be repaired.",false,"False. In the therapy research the book cites, the gap between the high initial distress of couples who had experienced infidelity and that of other couples could largely close by the end of treatment and at the six-month follow-up. The book adds that this doesn't mean “after cheating you must stay together”: it only shows that infidelity doesn't automatically make a relationship beyond repair."],["Behind cheating there may be not one single cause but a range of different motives.",true,"True. In a study by Selterman and colleagues with 495 people who had experienced infidelity, anger, feeling neglected, falling out of love, low commitment, a wish to boost self-esteem, and situational factors like stress emerged as separate motives. A large part of the sample were young adults, so the breakdown can't be generalised. The book also stresses that this doesn't remove responsibility: an explanation is not an excuse."]],"seviyeler":[[0,"Trusts the old sayings","Most of your beliefs sit close to the firm sentences of everyday talk: “Once a cheater, always a cheater”, “Happy people don't cheat.” The book shows that some of these sentences hold a grain of truth, but the data doesn't carry that certainty. Research usually talks about differences in risk, not destiny. Past behaviour can carry information about risk, but it doesn't stick a lifelong label on a person. The book's warning: “Assessing risk and condemning a person are not the same thing.”"],[3,"Sees the grey area","On some beliefs you're in the same place as the data, on others you're closer to the old sayings. That's exactly why cheating is a hard topic: people largely agree at the centre and split seriously at the edges. According to the book, when two people say “we're monogamous”, they may not have signed the same contract. Faithfulness is not just a list of things you can't do, it's a shared expectation about boundaries. Digital communication makes this grey area even bigger."],[5,"Economist of betrayal","You can tell common beliefs about cheating apart from the data. That's also where the book lands: the economic core of cheating is less about the number of partners and more about a secret departure from an agreed rule. So the real cost is not only the lost relationship but the decisions the other person made with missing information. That's why secrecy adds a separate cost on top of the act itself. Now the chapter's closing question: “Can trust be rebuilt once it's broken, and if so, which behaviours, how much time and what kind of mutual effort will pay the price?”"]]},{"no":28,"slug":"affetmek-guvenmek","ad":"Forgiving, trusting, compromising: where do you get stuck?","tur":"profil","bolum":"24","sezon":3,"acilis":"2026-12-24","kanca":"Three separate decisions are often mistaken for one. Where do you stand?","sorular":[["Someone close lied to you about something important and apologised. In the first few weeks, you…",[["F","Can't get past my anger, even hearing the apology is hard"],["G","Accept the apology, but weigh every word they say"],["U","I'm not angry, but I don't know how we'll go on"],["H","They apologised, so it's closed, I don't drag it out"]]],["For you, forgiving…",[["F","Feels like saying what they did was acceptable"],["G","Is possible, but trust is a separate account"],["U","Forgiving the person doesn't mean continuing the relationship"],["H","Means forgetting and moving on"]]],["If someone who shook your trust says “You should trust me now”…",[["F","I think they have no right to ask that"],["G","I say words aren't enough, I need to see their behaviour"],["U","Even if I trust them, we need to talk about how we go on"],["H","They may have a point, how long can we drag this out"]]],["A friend found out their partner hid a debt from them. What do you tell them?",[["F","“Your anger is completely natural, take your time.”"],["G","“From now on you might want to go through the accounts together.”"],["U","“Forgiving and staying together are separate decisions.”"],["H","“It's only money, it can be fixed, don't blow it up.”"]]],["In a process like this, what would be hardest for you?",[["F","Carrying what happened around inside me"],["G","Worst-case thoughts popping up whenever their phone rings"],["U","Acting as if everything is back to normal"],["H","The topic being brought up again and again"]]],["Which sentence feels closest to you?",[["F","Forgiveness has to be earned."],["G","I've forgiven, but it's not like before."],["U","Forgiving is one thing, staying is another."],["H","Holding a grudge wears you out first."]]]],"sonuclar":{"F":["Stuck at forgiving","For you, what happened doesn't close easily. The book doesn't see forgiveness as forgetting or as calling the act acceptable; it links it more to being able to step out of a cycle of constant retaliation and anger. Some studies have found a tendency to forgive linked with less negative conflict. But the book stresses this doesn't mean “forgive whatever they do”. And the person who broke the trust can't set the repair timetable on their own.","23"],"G":["Stuck at trusting again","You can forgive, but it takes time for trust to come back. The book treats these two as separate decisions anyway. Wanting more information after a breach is understandable, but temporary reassurance and permanent surveillance are not the same thing. In the book's image, training wheels help you learn to ride a bike, but the skill only settles once you can balance without them. Maybe the goal isn't the old trust, but a new trust built on new information.","24"],"U":["Stuck at reconciling","You can forgive the person, and even trust them again. Where you get stuck is on what terms the relationship will continue. The book draws a very clear line here: forgiving, trusting and reconciling are three separate decisions. Staying together isn't, on its own, proof of true forgiveness either. In economic language, forgiveness is not a past debt magically wiped from the books, but a new decision about the terms on which the relationship will go on.","23"],"H":["Quick to close","You don't like dragging out hurt feelings; once an apology comes, you turn the page. That can give a relationship flexibility. But the book points to a limit: in McNulty's study of newlywed couples, when problem behaviour was repeated often, being very forgiving didn't lead to good outcomes in every case. In the book's words, forgiveness being good doesn't make boundaries unnecessary. An apology is a signal; what comes next decides whether it can be trusted.","24"]}},{"no":29,"slug":"kriz-senaryolari","ad":"What would you do? 8 crisis scenarios","tur":"senaryo","bolum":"22","sezon":3,"acilis":"2026-12-28","kanca":"When a crisis hits, everyone's eyes drift somewhere different. Where do yours go?","sorular":[["You've been together for years and lately the same fight keeps coming back. One evening, alone, what's your first thought?",[["H","How much effort I've put into this relationship"],["G","Why they've changed, whether they're hiding something"],["A","What a different life could look like"],["B","Whether we've ever talked about what we expect from each other"],["C","What kind of life I'm living on my own two feet"]]],["You found out your partner has been having long chats with their ex without telling you.",[["H","I think, “After all our years, this?”"],["G","First I want to see all the messages"],["A","I think, so they're looking at other options too"],["B","I want to talk about whether this counts as cheating for us"],["C","I look at what I have of my own outside the relationship"]]],["Your partner confessed to a big purchase from the shared budget without telling you, and apologised.",[["H","I weigh the money spent against everything I've given so far"],["G","From now on I want to see every expense"],["A","I think someone else wouldn't have done this"],["B","We talk again about the rules we live by when it comes to money"],["C","I check how my own savings and income are doing"]]],["For a long time you've felt like you're the only one putting effort into the relationship.",[["H","A list of who gave what forms in my head"],["G","I try to figure out how willing they really are"],["A","I wonder if I'd go to this much trouble with someone else"],["B","I realise I never clearly said what I expect"],["C","I realise I've been neglecting my own friends and hobbies"]]],["Your partner got a good job offer in another city. You're at the best point of your career here.",[["H","We look at who has sacrificed for whose career so far"],["G","I wonder how long they've been thinking about this"],["A","Going and staying both mean giving something up, I weigh both"],["B","We put on the table what we each expect from this relationship"],["C","Whatever we decide, I look for a way to protect my own career"]]],["You're going through a hard time. A friend shows you a dating app and says, “Look how many options there are.”",[["H","Dropping all that investment and starting from zero feels too expensive"],["G","I say, who knows what the people on the screen are really like"],["A","I look with curiosity, maybe there really is someone better"],["B","I say I first need to know what I actually want"],["C","Knowing there are options is comforting, but I don't rush"]]],["Your partner said, “You don't understand me any more.”",[["H","I list the things I've done for them"],["G","I think about why they're saying it now and what's behind it"],["A","The thought crosses my mind that they might fit better with someone else"],["B","I ask what I expect from them and what they expect from me"],["C","I remind myself my life isn't only this relationship"]]],["You got through a crisis and people around you say, “You're back to your old selves.” And you?",[["H","I'm still calculating what this crisis cost us"],["G","I'll keep my eyes open for a while longer"],["A","Now and then I wonder what would have happened if we'd chosen differently"],["B","We're not like before, we have new rules now"],["C","I saw that keeping a space of my own helped me through it"]]]],"sonuclar":{"H":["The ledger","In a crisis, your first reflex is to weigh what was given and what was received. That isn't coldness: the book says the line “true love doesn't keep score” can sometimes hide inequality. But the ledger has a trap. Part of what was given in the past is sunk cost that can't be recovered, and it can carry too much weight in today's decision. Expecting an equal return for every act right away can also turn the relationship into an accounting book.","25"],"G":["The watcher","In a crisis, you need information first: what happened, why, what else is there? The book says wanting more transparency after a breach is understandable, but it separates temporary reassurance from permanent surveillance. Demands for information under surveillance have no natural end: when the phone comes up clean, you might start wondering about deleted messages. In the book's words: the alternative to trust is not perfect information.","24"],"A":["Weighing the alternatives","In a crisis, your mind wanders to roads not taken: another person, another city, another life. That's a natural way of seeing opportunity cost, and according to the book this awareness is sometimes genuinely useful. But the book reminds us this comparison isn't fair: we know our current partner's flaws, but we don't know the mistakes the person we passed up would make. In the book's words: the alternative life isn't cost-free, we just haven't lived its costs yet.","26"],"B":["Expectation writer","In a crisis, you ask whether the problem lies with the person or with the expectation. According to the book, an expectation gap isn't always the partner's fault: the expectation may be contradictory, out of line with resources, or never discussed at all. That's a strong style. The risk: treating every crisis as a matter of expectations can make you miss what is truly non-negotiable. As the book reminds us, rearranging expectations doesn't mean erasing basic boundaries.","28"],"C":["On your own feet","In a crisis, you want to be sure your own life is still standing: your job, your income, your friends. The book doesn't see this as preparing to escape: having more ability to leave is not the same as wanting to leave more. According to the book, being able to leave can even make staying more meaningful. The risk: if these supports replace shared investment, the relationship may feel less like “the chosen option” and more like “the option kept for now”.","22"]}},{"no":30,"slug":"kriz-aninda","ad":"In a crisis, are you on the same side?","tur":"cift","bolum":"24","sezon":3,"acilis":"2026-12-31","kanca":"Answer separately, then compare: when a crisis hits, do you run in the same direction?","sorular":[["After a big argument:",["Talk it through the same day","Sleep on it, then talk"]],["After something that shook trust, the priority is:",["Understanding why it happened","Talking about what comes next"]],["What makes an apology most believable:",["Saying it right away and clearly","Seeing it in behaviour over time"]],["After a crisis, phones and passwords:",["Stay open for a while","Everyone keeps their own space"]],["If a crisis breaks out over money:",["The rules get written down clearly","Trust-based flexibility is kept"]],["When discussing a hard topic:",["Everything goes on the table at once","Step by step, piece by piece"]],["Forgiving is:",["Closing the page completely","Keeping the record and moving on"]],["Once the crisis is over, the relationship should:",["Go back to how it was","Be rebuilt with new rules"]],["When debating whether to stay or go, what weighs more:",["The years spent together","The years ahead"]],["Support in hard times:",["Stays between the two of us","Is also shared with friends, family or a professional"]]],"seviyeler":[[0,"Different crisis languages","In a crisis, you stand in different places on most things. That doesn't mean your relationship is weak, it means you live through crises in different languages. The book sees a relationship as an incomplete contract: you can't write down every situation in advance, and not everything can be turned into a written rule. The gaps get filled by each other's goodwill, sense of fairness and past behaviour. The items where you answered differently may simply be items you haven't talked about yet."],[4,"Partial agreement","On some things you're on the same side, on others not. According to the book, trust isn't a single score but a set of expectations that differ by area. You can be in the same place on money and still differ on how you talk things through. Look at the items where you split, together. In the book's words, a solid relationship uses clear agreements and earned trust together. Trust is not an alternative to a contract; neither one is enough on its own."],[7,"On the same side","In a crisis, you stand in the same place on most things. This is the valuable side of what the book calls trust capital: when you don't have to renegotiate every small decision, the relationship's transaction costs fall. Still, the book gives a warning: a crisis doesn't automatically make a relationship stronger. Agreement on paper is a start; according to the book, trust feeds on behaviour."],[9,"Shared crisis map","You gave the same answer on almost every item. It looks like you share a map of what to do when a crisis hits. In the book's words, trust doesn't remove uncertainty, it lowers the cost of acting together within it. Your answers suggest this cost may be low for you, and that you fill the gaps in your incomplete contract in similar ways. The real test is still behaviour: according to the book, an apology is a signal. What comes next decides whether it can be trusted."]]},{"no":31,"slug":"tinder-oncesi-sonrasi","ad":"Do you love like it’s before Tinder, or after?","tur":"nesil","bolum":"30","sezon":4,"acilis":"2027-01-04","kanca":"Who used to introduce lovers: the neighbourhood, a friend or a screen? Which era do your instincts come from?","sorular":[["How would you like to meet the love of your life?",[["M","On the same street, at the same school or at a wedding"],["A","Through a friend who says “You two have to meet”"],["I","Through a connection that starts with long written messages"],["K","By popping up on my screen, a few kilometres away"],["Y","Through a suggestion from an app that knows me well"]]],["You’ve just met someone new. What do you check before you trust them?",[["M","Who knows their family and the people around them"],["A","What our mutual friend says about them"],["I","Whether their messages stay consistent over time"],["K","Whether their profile is verified"],["Y","The app’s compatibility score and suggestion"]]],["How big should your dating pool be?",[["M","The people I already know are enough; I don’t trust far-away strangers"],["A","As big as my friends’ friends"],["I","It should go beyond my city, even my country"],["K","The more profiles, the better"],["Y","Big, but narrowed down to a few good candidates"]]],["What tires you out the most?",[["M","Everyone knowing everything about everyone"],["A","Friends getting stuck in the middle after a break-up"],["I","Meeting face to face always getting postponed"],["K","Endless swiping through profiles"],["Y","Wondering how much of a message was really written by me"]]],["How do you make the first move?",[["M","No need for messages, I just say hello in person"],["A","I send word through a friend"],["I","With a long, careful message of several paragraphs"],["K","I write a quick joke and send it right away"],["Y","I get an AI to polish my draft first"]]],["Which sentence feels closest to you?",[["M","Your destiny is whoever lives nearby."],["A","A recommendation from someone you trust is worth its weight in gold."],["I","A good conversation can shrink any distance."],["K","More options mean a better chance of finding the right one."],["Y","The right data brings the right person."]]]],"sonuclar":{"M":["The Neighbourhood Era","Your love map is drawn around the people close to you: the same street, the same school, a table at a wedding. According to the book, for most of history geography largely decided how many partners a person could ever meet. In this world, family and neighbourhood were both the meeting place and a quiet trust check. The cost: how compatible you might have been with someone you never knew existed made no practical difference. Small pool, solid trust.","30"],"A":["The Friend-Bridge Era","For you, the most reliable way to meet someone is with a friend vouching for them. The book puts it like this: “I’ve known them for years” was never a perfect guarantee, but it could reduce the information problem between two strangers. In economics, this is an intermediation service. US data show that meeting online overtook meeting through friends around 2013. Your cost: your pool is only as big as your circle of friends.","30"],"I":["The Internet Era","You’re ready to go beyond geography, but you’re not in a rush. According to the book, trains, cars, universities and big cities widened the partner pool. But for a long time, the borders of the love market were mostly the borders of the physical world. In your era, strangers connect with each other, but they aren’t yet lined up on a phone screen. Your strength is the information that builds up over time in written conversation. The cost: no matter how detailed a profile is, it can’t carry a tone of voice, body language or the chemistry between two people.","30"],"K":["The Swipe Era","You’re a post-Tinder person: a big pool, quick decisions, left or right. According to the book, it’s not just the finger movement that changed. Part of the matchmaking that family, friends and neighbours used to do is now done by platforms. Your advantage is reaching people you would never meet otherwise. The cost: millions of profiles don’t mean millions of real options, because the other side has to choose you too. What’s scarce now is attention.","30"],"Y":["The AI Era","You’re one step ahead: you’re ready to hand the work of searching for a partner over to software. The book notes that this has already started: in March 2026 Tinder announced an AI-powered personalisation layer called “Chemistry”. The gain is clear: search costs fall. The cost is sneakier: a system that learns from your past choices may keep bringing you more of the same, and lower your chances of bumping into someone good by accident.","34"]}},{"no":32,"slug":"hangi-on-yil","ad":"Which decade does your love life belong to?","tur":"nesil","bolum":"29","sezon":4,"acilis":"2027-01-07","kanca":"Some people live by their idea of love, not by the calendar. Which decade is yours stuck in?","sorular":[["What comes to mind when you hear “marriage proposal”?",[["P","A stone that lasts a lifetime, a ring"],["U","Two lives setting up a system together"],["D","A promise made young, full of excitement"],["O","First messages, then a date, then a decision"],["Z","The final stone, placed once everything else is in place"]]],["When is the right time to get married?",[["P","When you’re sure it will last forever"],["U","When it’s clear who will take care of what"],["D","In your twenties, to build a life together"],["O","When we know each other well enough; age doesn’t matter"],["Z","When the job, the home and the savings are all sorted"]]],["What do you value most in a relationship?",[["P","Permanence, that it lasts forever"],["U","Division of labour, everyone knowing their job"],["D","Starting from zero together"],["O","Having chosen from a big pool"],["Z","Growing as people together"]]],["How would you like to have met your partner?",[["P","Someone from a very different world than mine"],["U","Someone who fits my way of life, introduced by someone we know"],["D","Introduced by a mutual friend"],["O","On an app, with no mutual friends at all"],["Z","From a few candidates suggested by AI"]]],["When it comes to money in a relationship…",[["P","A big gesture means a big commitment"],["U","One earns, one runs the home; both are work"],["D","You start with little money and grow it together"],["O","Everyone handles their own account; shared costs get discussed"],["Z","First you cross the financial threshold, then you get married"]]],["Which sentence feels closest to you?",[["P","Some things are forever."],["U","A good marriage is a good division of labour."],["D","First you marry, then you build."],["O","These days screens introduce lovers, not friends."],["Z","First you build, then you marry."]]]],"sonuclar":{"P":["1940s Love: Forever","You measure love by how long it lasts. According to the book, from 1939 onwards diamonds were systematically linked with romantic commitment. The slogan “A Diamond Is Forever”, written by Frances Gerety in 1947, became the centre of the campaign from the following year. In the same years, the tendency of similarly educated people in the US to marry each other was falling. The cost: a norm invented quite recently can look like “the way it has always been” just a few generations later.","31"],"U":["1950s Love: Division of Labour","You see a relationship as a well-run partnership: each person specialises in one area, and together you produce more. Gary Becker’s marriage model, published in 1973, describes exactly this. According to the book, the model captured the real structure of many households in the mid-twentieth century quite well. The cost: a description is not an unchangeable law. As education, employment and household technology changed, the returns to specialisation changed too.","15"],"D":["1990s Love: Marry First, Build Later","You believe in building a life together from scratch. According to the OECD comparison quoted in the book, in the early 1990s the average age at first marriage in OECD countries was 22–27 for women and 24–30 for men. Your model is close to what the book calls the old “cornerstone” model of marriage: you marry first, then build the home and savings together. Your risk is a financially fragile start. Your gain is the shared story of what you build together.","29"],"O":["2010s Love: No Middleman","You don’t need a go-between to meet someone. According to US data from Rosenfeld and colleagues, among heterosexual couples meeting online overtook meeting through friends around 2013. The researchers called this the “disintermediation” of dating. Your gain is a big pool. The cost: the small piece of trust information a friend used to provide disappears too. This finding is about the US; not every country changes at the same speed.","30"],"Z":["2020s Love: The Capstone","You’d rather build your life first and marry later. According to the OECD data in the book, by 2021 the average age at first marriage had risen to about 31 for women and about 33.4 for men. Sociologists call this “capstone marriage”: the wedding is placed on top of a life that is already built. The cost: as the threshold rises, people who are financially fragile may reach marriage later, or never. Marriage hasn’t lost its value; its entry requirements have become heavier.","29"]}},{"no":33,"slug":"kaydirma-ekonomisi","ad":"The swipe economy: 6 claims about dating apps","tur":"mit","bolum":"30","sezon":4,"acilis":"2027-01-11","kanca":"Everyone has a theory about dating apps. Which ones actually match the data?","sorular":[["In the US, meeting online has overtaken meeting through friends.",true,"True. According to nationally representative 2017 data from Rosenfeld and colleagues (2019), among heterosexual couples in the US, meeting online overtook meeting through friends around 2013. The book adds a warning: this finding is about the US and does not mean that the way people meet is changing at the same speed everywhere."],["Relationships that start on an app are unhappier and shorter than those that start offline.",false,"False. In Potarca’s (2020) study of 3,245 people in Switzerland, couples who met on a mobile app showed no clear disadvantage in relationship or life satisfaction. They also had stronger intentions to move in together. This doesn’t prove that app relationships are better either. The safer conclusion: the way you meet does not, on its own, decide the quality of the relationship."],["If there are millions of profiles on the screen, you have millions of options.",false,"False. A dating app is not a supermarket. In a supermarket, the product doesn’t have to choose you; in dating, the other person has to accept you too. As the book puts it, every user is both chooser and chosen at the same time."],["On apps, people tend to message users who rank, on average, as more “desirable” than themselves.",true,"True. In Bruch and Newman’s (2018) study in four large US cities, both women and men messaged users who ranked, on average, about 25 percent higher than themselves. The bigger the gap, the lower the chance of a reply. This ranking does not show a person’s worth, only their position on the platform."],["With paid features such as Boost, you can buy love itself.",false,"False. Money can affect how many people see your profile. But it can’t guarantee that the other person will be interested, or guarantee a match, a conversation or a relationship. According to the book, paid visibility affects access to the chance of being chosen, not love."],["In a field experiment, an algorithm that took both sides’ interest into account produced more mutual matches than a system that only tried to maximise clicks.",true,"True. In Chen and colleagues’ (2023) experiment with 690,857 accounts in Taiwan, the algorithm using two-sided logic spread visibility more widely and produced more mutual matches. The limit is also clear: what was measured was mutual likes, not happy relationships."]],"seviyeler":[[0,"Screen Illusion","Most of your instincts about dating apps didn’t match the data. That’s no surprise: the swipe interface makes romantic choice feel a lot like shopping. But as the book stresses, Tinder didn’t remove scarcity from the love market; it only changed some of the things that are scarce. Today, what’s scarce is not profiles but attention and mutual interest. Next time you swipe, remember: everyone on the screen is both chooser and chosen at the same time."],[3,"Mindful Swiper","You read some mechanisms correctly, but your instincts misled you on others. Here lies the great paradox of digital dating: abundance and scarcity are growing at the same time. We can see more potential partners than ever before in history, but, as the book reminds us, a day still has only twenty-four hours. Searching has become cheap, but evaluating people and the emotional work involved have not disappeared. The number of alternatives has grown, but the need for mutual interest is still there."],[5,"Platform Economist","You understand how platforms work: the need for mutual interest, the unequal spread of attention, the price of visibility. Now comes the hard question the book asks. “The most matches”, “the longest app use” and “the best long-term relationship” are not the same goal. Which one should an algorithm count as success? The book suggests asking “What is it trying to predict?” before asking “How accurately does it predict?”"]]},{"no":34,"slug":"ask-endustrisi","ad":"How much has the love industry sold you?","tur":"hesap","bolum":"31","sezon":4,"acilis":"2027-01-14","kanca":"Flowers, rings, weddings, posts. Do you express your love in your own words, or with a ready-made script?","sorular":[["Your partner did nothing on 14 February. What do you think?",[[0,"It’s an ordinary day; I don’t think anything of it"],[1,"A bit odd, but not important"],[2,"I’d be hurt; I was expecting something that day"],[3,"It means something is wrong in the relationship"]]],["What decides the value of a gift the most?",[[0,"That it shows how well they know me"],[1,"That it was thought through; the price comes second"],[2,"That it’s carefully chosen and from a good brand"],[3,"How much was spent on it"]]],["A marriage proposal and a ring?",[[0,"A ring isn’t necessary; a promise is enough"],[1,"A family ring or a simple one"],[2,"It should be a diamond, but at a reasonable price"],[3,"A diamond, at a price that does justice to their income"]]],["When you talk about the wedding budget, which sentence feels closest to you?",[[0,"A small civil ceremony; the rest is for our life together"],[1,"Our loved ones should come, but no showing off"],[2,"We only marry once; it can be a little extra"],[3,"We can’t fall behind the weddings people around us have had"]]],["You didn’t post about your anniversary on social media.",[[0,"We don’t post anyway"],[1,"Posting is nice, but not necessary"],[2,"If we don’t post, something feels missing"],[3,"I’d worry about what people around us will think"]]],["You saw a friend’s engagement ring.",[[0,"I hope they’re happy; I don’t compare"],[1,"I say it’s lovely and move on"],[2,"It pushes my expectations up a bit"],[3,"I compare it with what my partner will buy me"]]]],"seviyeler":[[0,"Your Own Dictionary","You mostly express your love in your own words. According to the book, this doesn’t have to mean you’re escaping the market, because the opposite of the market isn’t the absence of money. Your risk: while saying “I don’t measure love in money”, you may look down on rituals your partner cares about. The real issue isn’t money but paying attention to the other person’s world of meaning. This index is not an exact measurement; it’s a thinking tool to help you ask yourself questions."],[25,"Selective Consumer","You use the symbols the market offers, but not all of them. The same red rose can be a cliché for one person and the memory of a first date for another. That’s the book’s point: a standard product doesn’t have to produce a standard feeling. The question to ask is which of the expectations behind your symbols are really your own. This index is not a diagnosis; it’s a thinking tool for looking at your own choices."],[50,"Ritual Customer","Making love visible matters to you: the right day, the right gesture, the right gift. Rituals can truly add meaning to a relationship. But the book separates a shared ritual from a compulsory performance. Once the thought “If we don’t do this, people will think our relationship is failing” kicks in, the same gesture can turn into pressure from social judgement. The market supplies the tools of a ritual; the meaning the couple gives it decides whose ritual it is. This index is a thinking tool, not a verdict."],[75,"Script Subscriber","The ready-made script about how love should look runs strongly in you. You’re not alone: in the US, planned spending for Valentine’s Day 2026 was estimated at 29.1 billion dollars. But the book’s warning is clear: treating price as a perfect thermometer of feeling is a mistake. The book’s question: are you spending because you want to, or because you’re trying to keep up with a social minimum? This is a thinking tool."]],"birim":"Love Industry Index"},{"no":35,"slug":"yalnizligin-fiyati","ad":"What do you know about the price of loneliness?","tur":"mit","bolum":"33","sezon":4,"acilis":"2027-01-18","kanca":"Living alone, being alone, feeling lonely. Are they the same thing?","sorular":[["In Türkiye, the share of one-person households has clearly risen over the last decade.",true,"True. According to TurkStat (TÜİK), one-person households made up 13.9 percent of all households in 2014 and 20.5 percent in 2025. So today, roughly one in five households is a one-person household."],["This figure shows that roughly one in five people in Türkiye is lonely.",false,"False. TurkStat (TÜİK) isn’t measuring loneliness here; it’s measuring how many people live in the same household. Living alone is a household arrangement. Loneliness is a subjective experience that comes from the gap between the relationships you have and the relationships you want. You can be lonely in a crowded home, and you can live alone and still have strong bonds."],["Loneliness is mainly a problem of old age.",false,"False. According to the WHO’s 2025 assessment, roughly one in five people in younger age groups experiences loneliness. The OECD also found some of the sharpest declines in social connection indicators between 2018 and 2022 in the 16–24 age group."],["According to the World Health Organization, roughly one in six people worldwide experiences loneliness.",true,"True. The WHO’s global assessment, published in 2025, gives this estimate and notes that the rate can be higher among young people."],["Talking to an AI companion can reduce feelings of loneliness right afterwards.",true,"True, but read this carefully. In research by De Freitas and colleagues (2026), loneliness dropped right after use. One of the most important mechanisms was feeling listened to and heard. This result does not show that AI replaces friends, and it doesn’t measure long-term effects."],["It has been firmly proven that time spent on phones makes people lonely.",false,"False. According to the OECD’s 2025 assessment, many of the links between online interaction and loneliness are correlations. Lonely people using platforms differently could also explain them. What matters is less the amount of time than how it’s spent: a video call with your mother far away and silently scrolling through other people’s lives are not the same behaviour."]],"seviyeler":[[0,"Crowd Illusion","Most of your instincts about loneliness didn’t match the data. The most common mistake is using one word for three different things: social isolation, loneliness and living alone. As the book puts it, household statistics tell you how many people are inside the door. They don’t tell you who is outside when someone knocks. To understand loneliness, you need to look not at how many people you live with, but at who is truly present in your life."],[3,"Careful Observer","You made some distinctions correctly, but fell for an easy story on others. The most attractive explanations of loneliness are usually the neatest ones, like “phones made us lonely”. The book asks a harder question: “Which digital use, for which person, replaces or adds to which kind of relationship?” The answer varies from person to person, and that’s exactly what the data say."],[5,"Loneliness Economist","You read the economics of loneliness well: the difference between a household arrangement and a feeling, the picture among young people, the limits of the data. Here’s where the book lands: what’s scarce isn’t people. What’s scarce is time, attention, mutual interest, trust, accessible social spaces and repeated encounters. In the book’s words, an abundance of connections is not an abundance of relationships. The price of communication can approach zero, but attention stays scarce."]]},{"no":36,"slug":"yalniz-misin","ad":"Are you lonely, or have you chosen to be alone?","tur":"profil","bolum":"33","sezon":4,"acilis":"2027-01-21","kanca":"Is being on your own something missing, a choice, or just a scheduling problem?","sorular":[["It’s Friday evening and you have no plans. Your first thought?",[["B","Great, the house is mine, the evening is mine"],["Z","I’ll pop down to the local café; I’m bound to bump into someone I know"],["F","If only my friends and I could be free at the same time for once"],["P","I call the person closest to me; I don’t need anyone else"],["D","I open my phone and check what everyone is up to"]]],["Your car breaks down on the road. Who do you call?",[["B","The tow truck; I sort it out myself"],["Z","Someone I know nearby; there’s always somebody"],["F","Everyone I call is at work at that hour, but I try anyway"],["P","My partner or my one closest person"],["D","I post in a group chat: “Anyone nearby?”"]]],["What shrinks your social life the most?",[["B","Nothing; I’m as social as I want to be"],["Z","Fewer corner shops and fewer morning hellos"],["F","My working hours never match anyone else’s"],["P","Sharing everything with just one person"],["D","We message a lot but meet very little"]]],["Which describes you better?",[["B","I choose to be on my own and I enjoy it"],["Z","From the simit (Turkish sesame bread ring) seller to the neighbour, I chat with everyone"],["F","I love people, but my schedule won’t let me see them"],["P","I have one person, and that person is enough for me"],["D","I can reach hundreds of people with one tap"]]],["A friend’s birthday…",[["B","I remember it for the few people who really matter"],["Z","A short message to everyone; it’s a nice habit"],["F","I remember, but I never find time to call"],["P","Only one birthday really matters to me"],["D","If I get a notification, I write something"]]],["Which sentence feels closest to you?",[["B","Silence is my luxury."],["Z","Small hellos make big differences."],["F","Friendship takes time too."],["P","Once you find your soulmate, you don’t need anyone else."],["D","Everyone is just one message away."]]]],"sonuclar":{"B":["Chosen Silence","You experience being on your own not as something missing, but as a conscious choice. The book openly accepts this: personal space, independence, silence and full control over your time are very valuable gains for many people. Sometimes a one-person household is a freedom that was knowingly bought. The cost is economic: rent, bills and all the housework aren’t shared. Economists call this losing economies of scale.","33"],"Z":["Master of Weak Ties","You build your social life not only on a few close relationships, but also on small daily contacts: the street vendor, the neighbour, the familiar face at the gym. In Sandstrom and Dunn’s (2014) research, people reported more happiness and belonging on days when they interacted with more acquaintances than usual. The cost: because this network seems free, it’s easy to lose. A corner shop that closes can take a daily twenty-second hello with it.","33"],"F":["Time Poor","You stay distant not because you don’t want people, but because you can’t find free time that matches theirs. The book says it clearly: sometimes the biggest obstacle to human relationships isn’t conversation skills but coordination costs. Shifts, long commutes and care duties shrink social life without banning it. Friendship has maintenance costs too: social capital is not a savings account that earns interest by itself. Your risk is your bonds quietly wearing away.","33"],"P":["One-Stock Portfolio","Your social world is largely concentrated in one person. That bond may be very valuable. But the book warns in the language of finance: when friends, siblings and neighbours disappear, all your social risk sits with one person, which makes for a very concentrated portfolio. Expecting one person to be your lover, confidant, partner and entire social circle puts an enormous load on a relationship. Love can protect you from loneliness, but it isn’t a universal insurance policy.","33"],"D":["Connection-Rich","You can reach hundreds of people in a few seconds. The paradox the book sets out for our time describes you: the transaction cost of communication has fallen, but meaningful relationships are still scarce. The phone alone isn’t to blame. A video call and silently scrolling through other people’s lives are not the same behaviour. Your risk is confusing the number of people you can reach with real security. In the book’s words, digital visibility and social security are not the same currency.","33"]}},{"no":37,"slug":"ask-icin-ulke","ad":"Would you move to another country for love?","tur":"senaryo","bolum":"32","sezon":4,"acilis":"2027-01-25","kanca":"They say love knows no borders. Passports, languages and plane tickets say otherwise.","sorular":[["Your partner got a job offer in another country. Your first reaction?",[["G","I pack my bags; the rest will sort itself out"],["P","I’ll go, but we’ll discuss how we make up for my career loss together"],["K","I’ve built a life here; that life has to stay"],["U","They should go; we’ll manage long-distance for a while"],["M","We look for a third country that’s new for both of us"]]],["You’ve moved and don’t speak the local language. What do you do?",[["G","I sign up for a course right away and learn the language of the street"],["P","I learn their language, and they learn mine"],["K","I wouldn’t move, so I’d never end up in that situation"],["U","I won’t stay long; I’ll get by"],["M","At home we speak a shared language that is neither of our mother tongues"]]],["There’s Bayram (a Muslim religious holiday), and there’s Christmas. Where do you spend it this year?",[["G","With my partner’s family; I’ll learn new traditions"],["P","One year at theirs, one year at ours"],["K","At ours; I can’t imagine the holiday without my family"],["U","Everyone with their own family; we meet up afterwards"],["M","We take a bit from both and create our own tradition"]]],["In an argument, your partner says, “This is how it’s done in our culture.”",[["G","I try to adapt to their culture"],["P","Let’s do it their way on one thing and my way on another"],["K","I say it’s done differently in my culture"],["U","It’s not much of a problem while we’re apart; we’ll talk when we meet"],["M","I ask: “How will it be done in our home?”"]]],["If you have a child, which language will you speak at home?",[["G","The language of the country we live in"],["P","Each of us speaks our own mother tongue"],["K","My language, so they know their roots"],["U","First we need to figure out living in the same country"],["M","Three languages at once, one of them our shared language"]]],["What do you think is the biggest cost of moving for love?",[["G","There isn’t one; love is worth any price"],["P","The bill landing on one person’s career"],["K","Being cut off from my family and friends"],["U","Plane tickets, days off and time differences"],["M","Both sides giving up their habits"]]]],"sonuclar":{"G":["Nomad Heart","You’re ready to cross borders for love. Brave, but the book reminds you there’s a balance sheet: language, diplomas, professional licences and professional networks may not keep the same value when you change countries. Two people who start out economically equal can end up with a clear income gap within a few years. If moving was a joint decision but the cost is charged only to one person’s career, romantic sacrifice can slowly turn into economic dependence.","32"],"P":["Fair Negotiator","You see sacrifice not as a gesture without accounts, but as a line in a shared budget. That’s the book’s measure too: fairness in a long relationship isn’t about making equal sacrifices every day, but about whether costs are seen as the shared result of a shared decision. One partner may move in the early years; years later the roles may switch. Your risk: career and money are easy to count, but the work of adapting is easy to overlook. The book suggests also looking at who carries that work.","32"],"K":["Deep Roots","You don’t want to leave the place where you built your life. That’s understandable: your language, network and job market are your capital. The book shows the other side: the partner who knows the local language, law and job market controls the relationship’s connection to the outside world, while the migrant partner can become more dependent on them. Two languages in one home are a richness. Which one counts as the default also tells you something about the balance of power.","32"],"U":["Long-Distance Investor","You accept distance to protect both your career and your relationship. Technology is on your side: video calls and instant messages have made everyday what used to take weeks. But the book warns: as the cost of communication fell, the expectation of being available rose. Even not replying for a few hours can be read as a lack of interest. Plane tickets, visas, days off and time differences are still real. Distance doesn’t disappear; its cost structure changes.","32"],"M":["Architect of a Third Culture","You don’t want to copy either their culture or yours as it is. According to the book, healthy negotiation begins when you move from “This is how we do it” to “How will we do it in our relationship?” In Naeimi and Impett’s (2025) study of 592 people, some couples combined elements from both cultures to create practices of their own. The cost: rules other couples follow without ever discussing have to be talked through openly by you. Tiring, but it also brings hidden assumptions to light.","32"]}},{"no":38,"slug":"goruldu-ekonomisi","ad":"The “Seen” economy: what signal do your messages send?","tur":"senaryo","bolum":"06","sezon":4,"acilis":"2027-01-28","kanca":"It says “Seen”, but there’s no reply. Is that a signal, or just a long meeting?","sorular":[["Your message shows “Seen”, but there’s been no reply for three hours. You…",[["Z","Know they’ll write a long reply when they’re free"],["T","Check whether they always do this"],["S","Send another message; maybe I was misunderstood"],["M","Wait just as long myself, then write"],["C","Try to write my next message better"]]],["Which message moves you the most?",[["Z","A long voice message they made time for even on a busy day"],["T","A short “How was your day?” that arrives at the same time every evening"],["S","A long paragraph where they openly describe their feelings"],["M","A short but well-timed sentence that arrives when I least expect it"],["C","A witty, perfectly crafted message"]]],["You’re writing the first message to someone you like.",[["Z","A specific question that shows I read their profile carefully"],["T","A simple hello; what matters is what comes next"],["S","I write exactly what I feel without hiding anything"],["M","I keep it short so I don’t look too keen"],["C","I write and delete it many times, then send the most impressive version"]]],["You get a message saying “I miss you so much.” What do you think?",[["Z","Lovely, but are they making time to meet?"],["T","Have they shown it through their actions in recent weeks too?"],["S","I’m really happy; saying it matters"],["M","I let it wait a bit, then reply"],["C","I craft my reply carefully, with a beautiful sentence"]]],["Do you get help from AI when writing your messages?",[["Z","No, taking the time to write it myself is the whole point"],["T","No, people should know me as I really write"],["S","Maybe, if I can’t find the words for my feelings"],["M","No need, I don’t write much anyway"],["C","Yes, my messages come out smoother and funnier"]]],["Which sentence feels closest to you?",[["Z","Time is the gift you can never take back."],["T","Not once, but every day."],["S","Words left unsaid never exist."],["M","What is rare is valuable."],["C","A well-written message opens doors."]]]],"sonuclar":{"Z":["The Time Signal","You measure interest not by the length of a message, but by the time someone gives you. According to the book, time can be a strong signal because it can’t be taken back: money can be earned again, but a passing hour can’t be brought back. Time given by a very busy person can carry a high opportunity cost. Your risk: reading the size of a feeling from the amount of time. Without knowing the person’s alternatives and life circumstances, this calculation can easily go wrong.","06"],"T":["The Consistency Signal","You don’t look at a single message; you look at the pattern. The book calls this an everyday Bayesian calculation: as new information comes in, we update our beliefs about the other person. A single action is always open to interpretation; as the behaviour repeats, coincidental explanations get weaker. According to the book, one of the strongest signals in a long relationship isn’t showing off but consistency. Your risk: while waiting for a pattern to form, you may miss the first excitement and the first brave steps.","06"],"S":["The Words Signal","You aren’t shy about saying what you feel, and you care about what’s said. That openness is valuable. But the game-theory idea of “cheap talk” draws a line: cheap talk isn’t lying, but because someone who isn’t sincere can say the same sentence just as easily, words alone have limited power to tell people apart. The book’s formula: what’s said becomes credible as it’s backed up by actions and history.","06"],"M":["The Scarcity Signal","You deliberately limit how available you are: short messages, late replies. But the sender alone doesn’t decide what a signal means. According to the book, the meaning the sender intends and the meaning the receiver takes don’t have to be the same. What’s more, as communication got cheaper, the expectation of being available rose: even not replying for a few hours can be read as a lack of interest. Your “I’m valuable” message may be decoded on the other side as “they’re not interested”.","06"],"C":["The Cyrano Signal","You care about a message being well written, and you don’t mind getting help. The book doesn’t treat this as cheating in itself: photographers, hairdressers and friends’ advice are old helpers in presenting yourself. But AI lowers the cost of faking some signals. In Ante’s (2026) study, one of the main concerns was the jump from an assisted online persona to the unassisted person at a face-to-face meeting. The question is: is the person writing really you?","34"]}},{"no":39,"slug":"2040-aski","ad":"Are you ready for love in 2040?","tur":"profil","bolum":"34","sezon":4,"acilis":"2027-02-01","kanca":"The algorithm chooses, AI writes, and sometimes there isn’t even a human on the other side. Where do you stand in this future?","sorular":[["An app tells you, “This person is a 92 percent match.”",[["S","I trust it; data usually turn out to be right"],["Y","I open the profile and think about how to write to them"],["A","I let my AI assistant handle the first chat"],["P","The AI I talk to is already a perfect match for me"],["I","I take a face-to-face meeting seriously, not a percentage"]]],["It’s midnight and you want to pour your heart out.",[["S","I try talking to someone the app suggests"],["Y","I write to a friend, but first I tidy up my sentences with an AI"],["A","My assistant should find out who’s available at that hour"],["P","I talk to my AI companion; it’s always there"],["I","I wait until morning and call a friend"]]],["Which is more romantic to you?",[["S","A compatible stranger the algorithm found"],["Y","A perfectly crafted, witty message"],["A","Meeting three pre-screened candidates without wasting time"],["P","Someone who never rejects me and always understands"],["I","An imperfect but truly human handwritten note"]]],["What bothers you the most?",[["S","A compatible candidate I missed"],["Y","A badly written first message"],["A","First dates that go nowhere"],["P","Someone rejecting me one day"],["I","Finding out the person I’m chatting with is actually an AI"]]],["Your partner had an AI write the letter they gave you.",[["S","No problem; what matters is that they chose me"],["Y","I do it too; it’s perfectly normal"],["A","Efficient; they spent their time on something else"],["P","Doesn’t matter; beautiful sentences are beautiful"],["I","I’d be hurt; I wanted their own words"]]],["Which sentence feels closest to you?",[["S","The right data brings the right match."],["Y","The feeling is mine; the words just get some help."],["A","My time is valuable; let someone else do the screening."],["P","Whoever listens to me is whoever understands me."],["I","Being chosen is valuable because you might not be."]]]],"sonuclar":{"S":["Algorithm Believer","You’re ready to hand the work of finding a partner over to software. On the book’s ladder of AI in love, this is the first step: a system that ranks and recommends candidates. The gain is lower search costs. But the book’s warning matters: a system that learns whom you chose before may offer you ever-better copies of your past choices. Predicting what you will do and knowing what would be good for you to do are not the same problem.","34"],"Y":["The Cyrano Generation","You see the feeling as yours and the words as something a helper can polish. According to the book, using AI isn’t cheating in itself. But in Ante’s (2026) study, people who later found out the other person had used AI could feel deceived. As writing a lovely message gets cheaper, a lovely message may also lose its value as a signal. The question the book suggests isn’t “Did they use AI?” but “What did the AI do?”","34"],"A":["Agent Sender","To protect your time, you’re open to letting an AI agent do the pre-screening. The book finds this very attractive in economic terms: five candidates instead of a hundred profiles, three pre-filtered people instead of ten failed first dates. The cost is romantic serendipity, the chance of meeting a good person by accident. And the real question is clear: what did you tell your agent to count as success?","34"],"P":["Open to Synthetic Intimacy","You see the value of a companion who is always available and never keeps you waiting. The book takes this appeal seriously: an artificial partner dramatically lowers the risk of rejection and the cost of coordination. But it leaves a question: in a human relationship, the other person’s freedom to say “no” is part of what makes their “yes” valuable. And if the artificial partner is a company’s product, a single update can change the relationship.","34"],"I":["Analogue Romantic","You’re not against the future, but in love you want the human being themselves. According to the book, in the age of AI, verifiable signals of being human may become more valuable: meeting face to face, consistent behaviour, mutual friends. Just as a handwritten letter looks more romantic in the age of email, an imperfect but truly human sentence may become more precious. Your risk is rejecting useful tools wholesale too.","34"]}},{"no":40,"slug":"karne","ad":"Your Aşkonomi Report Card","tur":"karne","bolum":"","sezon":4,"acilis":"2027-02-01"},{"no":0,"slug":"ask-evresi","ad":"Which phase is your in-love brain in?","tur":"profil","bolum":"","sezon":0,"acilis":"2026-10-20","kanca":"Desire, attraction and attachment work like separate systems in the brain. Which one are you in?","sorular":[["When you think of them, what comes to mind first?",[["A","Closeness, wanting to touch"],["C","The next message, the next date"],["B","The everyday routine we’ve built together"]]],["How would you feel if you heard nothing from them for a whole day?",[["A","I’d miss them, but my life would carry on"],["C","I’d check my phone every minute"],["B","I’d wonder, but I wouldn’t worry. I trust them"]]],["How are your sleep and appetite lately?",[["A","Normal. My energy just jumps when I see them"],["C","I can’t sleep, and I forget to eat"],["B","Regular. I even sleep better next to them"]]],["What do you think about their flaws?",[["A","Haven’t really thought about it, the attraction is stronger"],["C","Flaws? I can’t see any yet"],["B","I know them all, and I’ve made peace with most"]]],["What do you love most when you’re together?",[["A","Physical closeness"],["C","My heart racing, that thrill"],["B","Being side by side without needing to talk"]]],["When a problem comes up between you, what’s your first reaction?",[["A","I take some distance, and come back if the pull is still there"],["C","My world falls apart, I want to fix it right now"],["B","We talk and find a way together"]]],["What do you talk about when you talk about the future?",[["A","We don’t really, we live in the moment"],["C","I dream about everything, but nothing is concrete"],["B","We make concrete plans: a home, work, holidays"]]],["If a friend described your relationship in one word, which would they pick?",[["A","Passionate"],["C","Head over heels"],["B","Peaceful"]]]],"sonuclar":{"A":["The desire phase","Right now you are pulled most by closeness and the body. Researchers treat sexual desire as a system linked to romantic attraction, but separate from it. Hormones such as testosterone play a big part in desire, in women as well as men. Desire alone tells you nothing about a relationship’s future. But it is the first spark of most stories. The real question is this: does this closeness grow into curiosity and attachment over time?",""],"C":["The attraction phase","You are right in the middle of it. Sleepless nights, a phone you keep checking, someone who looks flawless. Brain imaging studies show that in this phase the dopamine-driven reward and wanting system lights up clearly. Meanwhile, activity can drop in some areas linked to critical judgement of the loved one. Enjoy it. But it may be wise to put big decisions on hold for a while, because your brain is not giving you a neutral report right now.",""],"B":["The attachment phase","Calm seems to have replaced the thrill. That is not a decline, it is a different system taking over. Oxytocin and vasopressin are thought to play a role in attachment, although the strongest evidence comes from animal studies. The good news: some studies show the reward system can stay active even in long marriages. The capital of attachment is trust. Its maintenance is new experiences you share.",""]},"ozel":true},{"no":0,"slug":"askin-kimyasi-mitleri","ad":"Love’s chemistry: 6 claims, how many are true?","tur":"mit","bolum":"","sezon":0,"acilis":"2026-10-20","kanca":"Six sentences about love’s chemistry you hear everywhere. How many are backed by science?","sorular":[["Oxytocin is the “love hormone”: a nasal spray of oxytocin bonds people to each other.",false,"False. Oxytocin plays a role in childbirth, breastfeeding, touch and social bonding. But many studies on the behavioural effects of oxytocin nasal spray used small samples, and their results could not be reliably replicated (Walum, Waldman & Young, 2016). There is no single “love hormone”."],["The brain of someone who has been dumped but is still in love shows activity in areas linked to addiction.",true,"True. Brain scans of people who had recently been rejected but were still in love showed activity in areas linked to reward, motivation and addiction (Fisher et al., 2010). This does not mean love is an illness. But it helps explain why letting go is so hard."],["Love lasts three years at most. That is a biological rule.",false,"False. This sentence owes its fame more to pop culture than to science. People married for an average of twenty-one years who still reported intense romantic love showed reward-system activity in brain scans similar to that of new lovers (Acevedo et al., 2012). The shape of love can change. But it has no biological expiry date."],["Heartbreak can cause a real heart problem.",true,"True. Sudden, intense emotional stress can cause a temporary weakening of the heart muscle known as “broken heart syndrome”. These patients were found to have markedly high levels of stress hormones (Wittstein et al., 2005). In most cases it is temporary. But chest pain always needs urgent medical attention."],["When you are in love, your critical judgement of the loved one can weaken.",true,"True. In lovers looking at a photo of their partner, activity dropped in some brain areas linked to negative emotions and social judgement (Bartels & Zeki, 2004). People call this “love is blind”. The scientific finding is more limited, but it points the same way."],["Sexual desire and romantic attachment are the same thing and run on the same chemistry.",false,"False. Anthropologist Helen Fisher described lust, romantic attraction and attachment as linked but separate brain systems (Fisher, 1998). That is why a person can desire someone they feel no bond with, and feel a deep bond with someone they do not desire. The two can meet in the same relationship, but they are not the same thing."]],"seviyeler":[[0,"Slogan collector","Most of the popular sentences about love’s chemistry sounded convincing to you. You are not alone: these slogans are everywhere. The real picture is more complicated. There is no single “love hormone”; several brain systems work at the same time. And no chemical decides for you who you will build a life with."],[3,"Curious observer","You got some right and fell for the popular story on others. The most common mistake about love’s chemistry is reducing a complex process to a single molecule. The brain, the heart and hormones work together. But desire, attraction and attachment can also run separately."],[5,"Neuroscience reader","You can tell love’s chemistry apart from the slogans. The next step is this question: if chemistry tells us what we feel, how do we decide who to build a life with? That is exactly where Aşkonomi begins."]],"ozel":true}],"dil":"en"};
